[[:db/cas 17592186332715 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (y+11)^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=y+11.$ Then differentiating with respect to $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}y=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (y+11)^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {u}^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{8/3}}{8/3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(y+11)^{8/3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}] & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[(y+11)^{8/3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}(y+11)^{5/3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+11)+0 \\\\  & =(y+11)^{5/3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{5}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (y+11)^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=y+11.$ Then differentiating with respect to $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}y=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (y+11)^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {u}^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{8/3}}{8/3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(y+11)^{8/3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left[\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}\\right] & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[(y+11)^{8/3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}(y+11)^{5/3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+11)+0 \\\\  & =(y+11)^{5/3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{5}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332719 :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=2-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$, solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-3x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let $u=2-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$, solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-3x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332722 :question/worked-solution "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrr} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 3 & 3 & -9 & 1 & -1 \\\\  & ↓ & 9 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\hline  & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-1}{x-3}=3{x}^{2}+1+\\frac{2}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+1+\\frac{2}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrr} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 3 & 3 & -9 & 1 & -1 \\\\  & ↓ & 9 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\hline  & 3 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-1}{x-3}=3{x}^{2}+1+\\frac{2}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(3{x}^{2}+1+\\frac{2}{x-3}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332723 :question/worked-solution "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rrr} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & 10 \\\\  & ↓ & -5 & -10 \\\\ \\hline  & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+10}{x+5}=x+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+10}{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+2x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}]+2[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rrr} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & 10 \\\\  & ↓ & -5 & -10 \\\\ \\hline  & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+10}{x+5}=x+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+10}{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+2x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}\\right]+2[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332725 :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x+2}{2(x-\\frac{3}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{x+2}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rr} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 2 \\\\  & ↓ & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\hline  & 1 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})} & =1+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{7}{2(x-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{7}{2x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1+\\frac{7}{2x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\int \\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[x+\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x+2}{2\\left(x-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{x+2}{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rr} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 2 \\\\  & ↓ & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\hline  & 1 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} & =1+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{7}{2\\left(x-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{7}{2x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\left(1+\\frac{7}{2x-3}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\int \\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[x+\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|\\right]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332726 :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $3$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x+1}{3(x+\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{x+1}{(x+\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rr} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 1 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\hline  & 1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{(x+\\frac{2}{3})} & =1+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{(x+\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{3(x+\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{3x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int (1+\\frac{1}{3x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\int \\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[x+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we pull out the factor of $3$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x+1}{3\\left(x+\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{x+1}{\\left(x+\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rr} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 1 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\hline  & 1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{\\left(x+\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} & =1+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{\\left(x+\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{3\\left(x+\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{3x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\left(1+\\frac{1}{3x+2}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left[\\int \\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left[x+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|\\right]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332729 :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{4|x|\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|4x|\\sqrt{(4x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $4x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{4|x|\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|4x|\\sqrt{(4x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec} (4x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{4|x|\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|4x|\\sqrt{(4x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $4x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{4|x|\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|4x|\\sqrt{(4x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec} (4x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332730 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{1}{3+2x}+\\frac{1}{3-2x})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\left(\\frac{1}{3+2x}+\\frac{1}{3-2x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186332730 :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{3+2x}+\\frac{1}{3-2x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{3+2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{3-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3+2x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{3+2x}{3-2x}|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{3+2x}{3-2x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm." "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{3+2x}+\\frac{1}{3-2x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{3+2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{3-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3+2x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{3+2x}{3-2x}|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(K|\\frac{3+2x}{3-2x}|\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."] [:db/cas 17592186332731 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{1}{1+5x}+\\frac{1}{1-5x})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\left(\\frac{1}{1+5x}+\\frac{1}{1-5x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186332731 :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{1+5x}+\\frac{1}{1-5x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+5x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{1-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+5x|-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-5x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1+5x}{1-5x}|+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{1+5x}{1-5x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm." "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{1+5x}+\\frac{1}{1-5x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+5x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{1-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+5x|-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-5x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1+5x}{1-5x}|+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(K|\\frac{1+5x}{1-5x}|\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."] [:db/cas 17592186332732 :question/worked-solution "Since $u=2x+1$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{1}{2}(u-1).$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt[3]{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(u-1)(\\sqrt[3]{u})(\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int (u-1)({u}^{1/3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int ({u}^{4/3}-{u}^{1/3})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $u=2x+1$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{1}{2}(u-1).$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt[3]{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(u-1)(\\sqrt[3]{u})\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int (u-1)({u}^{1/3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int ({u}^{4/3}-{u}^{1/3})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332735 :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(4x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-16{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(4x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(4x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332736 :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1+(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1+(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332740 :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|3x|\\sqrt{(3x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $3x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|3x|\\sqrt{(3x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec} (3x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|3x|\\sqrt{(3x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $3x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3|x|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|3x|\\sqrt{(3x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec} (3x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332741 :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{6|x|\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $6x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{6|x|\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec} (6x)}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{6|x|\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $6x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{6|x|\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec} (6x)}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332759 :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into  the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /6}+{\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & +\\frac{2}{9}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[0-0]+\\frac{2}{9}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into  the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\right)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /6}+{\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\left[\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)\\right] \\\\  & +\\frac{2}{9}\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)\\right] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[0-0]+\\frac{2}{9}\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332760 :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]+\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}]+\\frac{1}{4}[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[0]+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right]+\\frac{1}{4}\\left[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right]+\\frac{1}{4}[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[0]+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332761 :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}+{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)] \\\\  & +[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]+[1-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}+{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & +\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right]+\\left[1-\\frac{1}{2}\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332762 :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}(x{e}^{2x}){|}_{0}^{1/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x{e}^{2x}){|}_{0}^{1/2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1/2)}-0\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 0}]-\\frac{1}{4}[{e}^{2(1/2)}-{e}^{2\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{e}{2}-0]-\\frac{1}{4}[e-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{4}-\\frac{e-1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-e+1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}(x{e}^{2x}){|}_{0}^{1/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x{e}^{2x}){|}_{0}^{1/2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{4}{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1/2)}-0\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 0}\\right]-\\frac{1}{4}[{e}^{2(1/2)}-{e}^{2\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\frac{e}{2}-0\\right]-\\frac{1}{4}[e-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{4}-\\frac{e-1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-e+1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332763 :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⇒ & v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & +2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}]+2[0-0] \\\\  & =2(-2\\pi )+0 \\\\  & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⇒ & v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2\\left[\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & +2\\left[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =2\\left[-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right]+2[0-0] \\\\  & =2(-2\\pi )+0 \\\\  & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332764 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}-25 & =25[\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}-1] \\\\  & =25[{\\left(\\frac{x+2}{5}\\right)}^{2}-1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{25[((x+2)/5)^{2}-1]}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\cdot \\sqrt{25}\\cdot \\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\cdot 5\\cdot \\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{15}{|x+2|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|(x+2)/5|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\frac{x+2}{5}.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x+2}{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|(x+2)/5|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =15\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} \\left(\\frac{x+2}{5}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}-25 & =25\\left[\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}-1\\right] \\\\  & =25\\left[{\\left(\\frac{x+2}{5}\\right)}^{2}-1\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{25[((x+2)/5)^{2}-1]}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\cdot \\sqrt{25}\\cdot \\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\cdot 5\\cdot \\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{15}{|x+2|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|(x+2)/5|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\frac{x+2}{5}.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x+2}{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|(x+2)/5|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =15\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} \\left(\\frac{x+2}{5}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332765 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\int }_{1}^{e}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}[uv{|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)(x){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}[e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}[e-x{|}_{1}^{e}\\,] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}(e-e+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\log }_{4}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\int }_{1}^{e}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}[uv{|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\left[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)(x){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}[e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}[e-x{|}_{1}^{e}\\,] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}(e-e+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332766 :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dx=-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right){|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4})\\left(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\left(-\\frac{1}{1}\\right)]+{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\frac{1}{x}{|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}}-(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1-5{e}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dx=-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right){|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4})\\left(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\left(-\\frac{1}{1}\\right)\\right]+{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\frac{1}{x}{|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}}-\\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{1}\\right) \\\\  & =1-5{e}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332771 :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4=(x-2)(x+2).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{2x+6}{(x-2)(x+2)}=\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+6=A(x-2)+B(x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+6 & =A(2-2)+B(2+2) \\\\ 10 & =4B \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+6 & =A(-2-2)+B(-2+2) \\\\ 2 & =-4A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}=-\\frac{1}{2(x+2)}+\\frac{5}{2(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2(x+2)}+\\frac{5}{2(x-2)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)^{5}|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)^{5}|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{x+2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{x+2}|{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-\\frac{1}{3}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-{3}^{5}|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{-1})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4=(x-2)(x+2).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{2x+6}{(x-2)(x+2)}=\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+6=A(x-2)+B(x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+6 & =A(2-2)+B(2+2) \\\\ 10 & =4B \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+6 & =A(-2-2)+B(-2+2) \\\\ 2 & =-4A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}=-\\frac{1}{2(x+2)}+\\frac{5}{2(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{2(x+2)}+\\frac{5}{2(x-2)}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)^{5}|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)^{5}|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{x+2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x-2)^{5}}{x+2}|{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-\\frac{1}{3}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-{3}^{5}|\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{-1})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332772 :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}=\\frac{A}{(x+7)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x+29=A(x+4)+B(x+7).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4)+29 & =A(-4+4)+B(-4+7) \\\\ 9 & =3B \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-7,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-7)+29 & =A(-7+4)+B(-7+7) \\\\ -6 & =-3A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}=\\frac{2}{(x+7)}+\\frac{3}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x+7}+\\frac{3}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x+7}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+7|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)^{3}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)^{3}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|{|}_{-6}^{-5} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}=\\frac{A}{(x+7)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x+29=A(x+4)+B(x+7).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4)+29 & =A(-4+4)+B(-4+7) \\\\ 9 & =3B \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-7,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-7)+29 & =A(-7+4)+B(-7+7) \\\\ -6 & =-3A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}=\\frac{2}{(x+7)}+\\frac{3}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{x+7}+\\frac{3}{x+4}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x+7}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+7|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)^{3}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)^{3}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|{|}_{-6}^{-5} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332773 :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)} & =\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x+8)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n11x+46=A(x+8)+B(x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-8,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11(-8)+46 & =A(-8+8)+B(-8+2) \\\\ -42 & =-6B \\\\ B & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11(-2)+46 & =A(-2+8)+B(-2+2) \\\\ 24 & =6A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)}+\\frac{7}{(x+8)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{x+2}+\\frac{7}{x+8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{x+8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n            \n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{4}(x+8)^{7}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-7}^{-3}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{4}(x+8)^{7}|{|}_{-7}^{-3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{7})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{4}) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)} & =\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x+8)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n11x+46=A(x+8)+B(x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-8,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11(-8)+46 & =A(-8+8)+B(-8+2) \\\\ -42 & =-6B \\\\ B & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11(-2)+46 & =A(-2+8)+B(-2+2) \\\\ 24 & =6A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)}+\\frac{7}{(x+8)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{4}{x+2}+\\frac{7}{x+8}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{x+8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n            \n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+8|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{4}(x+8)^{7}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-7}^{-3}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{4}(x+8)^{7}|{|}_{-7}^{-3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{7})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{4}) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332774 :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx+1=A(x+3)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+1 & =A(-3+3)+B(-3) \\\\ -2 & =-3B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+1 & =A(0+3)+B(0) \\\\ 1 & =3A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx+1=A(x+3)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+1 & =A(-3+3)+B(-3) \\\\ -2 & =-3B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+1 & =A(0+3)+B(0) \\\\ 1 & =3A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332775 :question/worked-solution "We rewrite our integral and integrate term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{3x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{23}{3(2x+3)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{23}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{23}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We rewrite our integral and integrate term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{4}{3x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{23}{3(2x+3)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{23}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{23}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332778 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD={1}^{2}-4(1)(1)=-3<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x({x}^{2}+x+1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+2x+1=A({x}^{2}+x+1)+(Bx+C)x.\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=2,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+x+1.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+x+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD={1}^{2}-4(1)(1)=-3<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x({x}^{2}+x+1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+2x+1=A({x}^{2}+x+1)+(Bx+C)x.\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=2,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+x+1.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+x+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332779 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-4$ and $x=8.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/56/56ec4a3e3bd4b8b652832d4b32ba730717a49ab2438ef2c978ea99b291a7f462.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $f(x)=x+6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}[\\,(x+6)-(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-4$ and $x=8.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/56/56ec4a3e3bd4b8b652832d4b32ba730717a49ab2438ef2c978ea99b291a7f462.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $f(x)=x+6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}\\left[\\,(x+6)-\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2\\right)\\,\\right]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+x+8\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332780 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,1):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-1)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,1]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-1)^{+}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,1):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-1)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,1]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-1)^{+}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}\\left({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332783 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}=\\frac{A}{(x-4)}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3-x)}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-4)^{2}(3-x)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9x+21=A(x-4)(3-x)+B(3-x)+C(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2}-9(4)+21 & =A(4-4)(3-4)+B(3-4)+C(4-4)^{2} \\\\ 16-36+21 & =B(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}-9(3)+21 & =A(3-4)(3-3)+B(3-3)+C(3-4)^{2} \\\\ 9-27+21 & =C{(-1)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{1} \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-9(0)+21 & =A(0-4)(3-0)+B(3-0)+C(0-4)^{2} \\\\ 21 & =-12A+3B+16C \\\\ 21 & =-12A+3(-1)+16(3) \\\\ 21 & =-12A+45 \\\\ 12A & =45-21 \\\\ A & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}=\\frac{2}{(x-4)}-\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(3-x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{5}^{6}(\\frac{2}{(x-4)}-\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(3-x)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{1}{3-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|{|}_{5}^{6}+\\frac{1}{x-4}{|}_{5}^{6}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-x|{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6-4|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-4|)+(\\frac{1}{6-4}-\\frac{1}{5-4})-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-6|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-5|) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}=\\frac{A}{(x-4)}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(3-x)}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-4)^{2}(3-x)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9x+21=A(x-4)(3-x)+B(3-x)+C(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2}-9(4)+21 & =A(4-4)(3-4)+B(3-4)+C(4-4)^{2} \\\\ 16-36+21 & =B(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}-9(3)+21 & =A(3-4)(3-3)+B(3-3)+C(3-4)^{2} \\\\ 9-27+21 & =C{(-1)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{1} \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-9(0)+21 & =A(0-4)(3-0)+B(3-0)+C(0-4)^{2} \\\\ 21 & =-12A+3B+16C \\\\ 21 & =-12A+3(-1)+16(3) \\\\ 21 & =-12A+45 \\\\ 12A & =45-21 \\\\ A & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}=\\frac{2}{(x-4)}-\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(3-x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{5}^{6}\\left(\\frac{2}{(x-4)}-\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(3-x)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{1}{3-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|{|}_{5}^{6}+\\frac{1}{x-4}{|}_{5}^{6}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-x|{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|6-4|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-4|)+\\left(\\frac{1}{6-4}-\\frac{1}{5-4}\\right)-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-6|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-5|) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332784 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}} & =\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n4x=A(x-2)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2) & =A(2-2)+B \\\\ 8 & =0+B \\\\ B & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0) & =A(0-2)+B \\\\ 0 & =-2A+B \\\\ 0 & =-2A+8 \\\\ A & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{(x-2)}+\\frac{8}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(\\frac{4}{(x-2)}+\\frac{8}{(x-2)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+8{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|{|}_{3}^{4}+8\\left(-\\frac{1}{x-2}\\right){|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-2|)+8(-\\frac{1}{4-2}+\\frac{1}{3-2}) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-2|)+8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}} & =\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n4x=A(x-2)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2) & =A(2-2)+B \\\\ 8 & =0+B \\\\ B & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0) & =A(0-2)+B \\\\ 0 & =-2A+B \\\\ 0 & =-2A+8 \\\\ A & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{(x-2)}+\\frac{8}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\left(\\frac{4}{(x-2)}+\\frac{8}{(x-2)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+8{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|{|}_{3}^{4}+8\\left(-\\frac{1}{x-2}\\right){|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-2|)+8\\left(-\\frac{1}{4-2}+\\frac{1}{3-2}\\right) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-2|)+8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332785 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n6x+12=A(x+3)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(-3)+12 & =A(-3+3)+B(-3) \\\\ -6 & =-3B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(0)+12 & =A(0+3)+B(0) \\\\ 12 & =3A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)}=\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{2}{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{2}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n6x+12=A(x+3)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(-3)+12 & =A(-3+3)+B(-3) \\\\ -6 & =-3B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(0)+12 & =A(0+3)+B(0) \\\\ 12 & =3A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)}=\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{2}{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{2}{x+3}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332786 :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x-1=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1 & =A(2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3)-1 & =A(2(3)+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 14 & =7A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{2x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x-1=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1 & =A\\left(2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1\\right)+B\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}-3\\right) \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3)-1 & =A(2(3)+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 14 & =7A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332787 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+x=x(3x+1).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(3x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{(3x+1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2=A(3x+1)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A(3\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+1)+B\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ 2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}B \\\\ 6 & =-B \\\\ B & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A(3(0)+1)+B(0) \\\\ 2 & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(3x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{3x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x(3x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{3x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{3}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+x=x(3x+1).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(3x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{(3x+1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2=A(3x+1)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A\\left(3\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+1\\right)+B\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ 2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}B \\\\ 6 & =-B \\\\ B & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A(3(0)+1)+B(0) \\\\ 2 & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(3x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{3x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x(3x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{3x+1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{3}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332791 :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{2}{3}t & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\frac{2}{3}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(3)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\frac{2}{3}t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{2}+C & =4 \\\\ 0+0+C & =4\\,⟹\\,C=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+4.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(3)=2(3)-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{2}+4=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$" "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{2}{3}t & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(2-\\frac{2}{3}t\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(3)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\left(2-\\frac{2}{3}t\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{2}+C & =4 \\\\ 0+0+C & =4\\,⟹\\,C=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+4.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(3)=2(3)-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{2}+4=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332793 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2(\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{{e}^{-2(-\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi }}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\right){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2(\\pi /2)}}{5}\\left(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right)+\\frac{{e}^{-2(-\\pi /2)}}{5}\\left(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi }}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332794 :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t),\n$$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t))}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\csc }^{2}⁡(2t)}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\csc }^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /12} & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\csc }^{3}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t),\n$$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t)\\right)}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\csc }^{2}⁡(2t)}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\csc }^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /12} & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\csc }^{3}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332797 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by ${x}^{2}(2x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =Ax(2x+3)+B(2x+3)+C{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =A(0)(2(0)+3)+B(2(0)+3)+C(0)^{2} \\\\ 2 & =3B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}+2 & =A\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)(2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+3)+B(2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+3)+C{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =C\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\ C & =\\frac{4}{18} \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=1$ and using our values for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+2 & =A(1)(2(1)+3)+\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(2(1)+3)+\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)(1)^{2} \\\\ 3 & =5A+\\frac{10}{3}+\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 3 & =5A+\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ 5A & =-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B\\cdot C & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{243}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by ${x}^{2}(2x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =Ax(2x+3)+B(2x+3)+C{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =A(0)(2(0)+3)+B(2(0)+3)+C(0)^{2} \\\\ 2 & =3B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}+2 & =A\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+3\\right)+B\\left(2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+3\\right)+C{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =C\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\ C & =\\frac{4}{18} \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=1$ and using our values for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+2 & =A(1)(2(1)+3)+\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(2(1)+3)+\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)(1)^{2} \\\\ 3 & =5A+\\frac{10}{3}+\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 3 & =5A+\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ 5A & =-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B\\cdot C & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{243}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332810 :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each componentt separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}⟨4t-1,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{1}^{4}(4t-1)\\,\\text{d}t,{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(2{t}^{2}-t){|}_{1}^{4},\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}){|}_{1}^{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(2(4)^{2}-4)-(2(1)^{2}-1),\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨27,\\,\\frac{14}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each componentt separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}⟨4t-1,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{1}^{4}(4t-1)\\,\\text{d}t,{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle (2{t}^{2}-t){|}_{1}^{4},\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\right){|}_{1}^{4}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle (2(4)^{2}-4)-(2(1)^{2}-1),\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 27,\\,\\frac{14}{3}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332815 :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3{e}^{t+2}}{6{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t})}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{2-2t}}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3{e}^{t+2}}{6{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t}\\right)}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{2-2t}}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332823 :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5}^{1}(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x){|}_{-5}^{1}+3x{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}+2(1)-\\frac{(-5)^{2}}{2}-2(-5)]+[3(3)-3(1)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+2-\\frac{25}{2}+10]+[9-3] \\\\  & =0+6 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5}^{1}(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x\\right){|}_{-5}^{1}+3x{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}+2(1)-\\frac{(-5)^{2}}{2}-2(-5)\\right]+[3(3)-3(1)] \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{2}+2-\\frac{25}{2}+10\\right]+[9-3] \\\\  & =0+6 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332840 :question/worked-solution "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2n}{n+2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have $\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$ converges." "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2n}{n+2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2}{\\left(1+\\frac{2}{n}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have $\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$ converges."] [:db/cas 17592186332848 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡u}{2}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡u}{2}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}-1\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332849 :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x={\\cos }^{2}⁡2x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}({\\cos }^{2}⁡2x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{4}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /24} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x={\\cos }^{2}⁡2x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}({\\cos }^{2}⁡2x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{4}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /24} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332856 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-2)^{2}(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+31 & =A(x-2)(2x+1)+B(2x+1)+C(x-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+31 & =A(-\\frac{1}{2}-2)(2[-\\frac{1}{2}]+1)+B(2[-\\frac{1}{2}]+1)+C{(-\\frac{1}{2}-2)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+31 & =A\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)(-1+1)+B(-1+1)+C\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{125}{4} & =\\frac{25C}{4} \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}+31 & =A(2-2)(2\\cdot 2+1)+B(2\\cdot 2+1)+C(2-2)^{2} \\\\ 35 & =5B \\\\ B & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=0$ and using our values for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+31 & =A(0-2)(2\\cdot 0+1)+7(2\\cdot 0+1)+5(0-2)^{2} \\\\ 31 & =-2A+7+20 \\\\ 31 & =-2A+27 \\\\ 2A & =-4 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nHence,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B\\cdot C=(-2)\\cdot 7\\cdot 5=-70.\n$$" "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-2)^{2}(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+31 & =A(x-2)(2x+1)+B(2x+1)+C(x-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+31 & =A\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}-2\\right)\\left(2\\left[-\\frac{1}{2}\\right]+1\\right)+B\\left(2\\left[-\\frac{1}{2}\\right]+1\\right)+C{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}-2\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+31 & =A\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)(-1+1)+B(-1+1)+C\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{125}{4} & =\\frac{25C}{4} \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}+31 & =A(2-2)(2\\cdot 2+1)+B(2\\cdot 2+1)+C(2-2)^{2} \\\\ 35 & =5B \\\\ B & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=0$ and using our values for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+31 & =A(0-2)(2\\cdot 0+1)+7(2\\cdot 0+1)+5(0-2)^{2} \\\\ 31 & =-2A+7+20 \\\\ 31 & =-2A+27 \\\\ 2A & =-4 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nHence,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B\\cdot C=(-2)\\cdot 7\\cdot 5=-70.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332868 :question/problem "![](images/87/87dc32f8909ac1c05adc981b4133a3f6e721918e4aca6923b08a073d2fb8cfe9.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ as shown above?" "![](images/87/87dc32f8909ac1c05adc981b4133a3f6e721918e4aca6923b08a073d2fb8cfe9.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ on $\\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right],$ as shown above?"] [:db/cas 17592186332870 :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(1),1+(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\left\\langle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 0-\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(1),1+(0)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332872 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{8}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{-1}+8{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1})+8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-{y}^{-2})+8\\cdot (-2{y}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-2{y}^{-2}-16{y}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{16}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2y-16}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y+16}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2y+16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,2),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,2)} & =-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{2(2)+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{20} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{8}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{-1}+8{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1})+8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-{y}^{-2})+8\\cdot (-2{y}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-2{y}^{-2}-16{y}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{16}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2y-16}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y+16}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2y+16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,2),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,2)} & =-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{2(2)+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{20} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332876 :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-1.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩.\n$$" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\left\\langle -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{C}_{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-1.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332891 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2$. So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{3}{2}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}) & =2((x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2$. So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2\\left({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{3}{2}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =2\\left((x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332892 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6$. So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x) & =6({(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}) \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6$. So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6\\left({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left(x-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left(x-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\left({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\\right) & =6\\left({\\left(x-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}\\right) \\\\  & =6{\\left(x-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & =6{\\left(x-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332896 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{3})?\n$$" "What is the amplitude of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{3}\\right)?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332899 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ for the curve $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)=\\sqrt{2}.$" "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ for the curve $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)=\\sqrt{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332899 :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x}\\right)}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x}\\right)}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332904 :question/worked-solution "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can calculate the coordinates of the center using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3b+(-3b)}{2},\\frac{6b+(-2b)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{0}{2},\\frac{4b}{2}) \\\\  & =(0,2b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius can be found by calculating the distance between the center $(0,2b)$ and a point on the circle, such as $A(3b,6b):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-3b)^{2}+(2b-6b)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3b)^{2}+(-4b)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{b}^{2}+16{b}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{b}^{2}} \\\\  & =5|b|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $b>0,$ we have that \n\n$$\nr=5|b|=5b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the circle is at $(0,2b)$ and the radius is $5b.$" "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can calculate the coordinates of the center using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =\\left(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3b+(-3b)}{2},\\frac{6b+(-2b)}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{0}{2},\\frac{4b}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =(0,2b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius can be found by calculating the distance between the center $(0,2b)$ and a point on the circle, such as $A(3b,6b):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-3b)^{2}+(2b-6b)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3b)^{2}+(-4b)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{b}^{2}+16{b}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{b}^{2}} \\\\  & =5|b|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $b>0,$ we have that \n\n$$\nr=5|b|=5b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the circle is at $(0,2b)$ and the radius is $5b.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332915 :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{2}-4x{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3})-4(2+2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\frac{32}{3}.$" "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{2}-4x{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}\\right)-4(2+2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\frac{32}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332921 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=0$ and $x=2$ is $x=1.$\n\nSince $1\\in [0,2]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{1}{5}-1]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [1,2]$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x)|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =([\\frac{32}{5}-2]-[\\frac{1}{5}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{26}{5}=\\frac{30}{5}=6.\n$$" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=0$ and $x=2$ is $x=1.$\n\nSince $1\\in [0,2]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x\\right)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\left[\\frac{1}{5}-1\\right]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [1,2]$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x\\right)|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left[\\frac{32}{5}-2\\right]-\\left[\\frac{1}{5}-1\\right]\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{26}{5}=\\frac{30}{5}=6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332945 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{4/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{4/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{4/5}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left[\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{4/5}\\right]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{4/5}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{4/5}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."] [:db/cas 17592186332946 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{6}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{6}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{6}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\left({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{6}|}_{a}^{0}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332955 :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{x}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+\\frac{1}{8}(4)^{2}]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{x}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}\\right)|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\left[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+\\frac{1}{8}(4)^{2}\\right]-\\left[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{2}\\right] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332962 :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x & =-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x & =-1 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}x) & =-1 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}x+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 3[{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-3\\cdot \\frac{4}{9} & =-1 \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3} & =-1 \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{3}.$" "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x & =-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x & =-1 \\\\ 3\\left({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}x\\right) & =-1 \\\\ 3\\left[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}x+{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\right] & =-1 \\\\ 3\\left[{\\left(x-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}\\right] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\left(x-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}-3\\cdot \\frac{4}{9} & =-1 \\\\ 3{\\left(x-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3} & =-1 \\\\ 3{\\left(x-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3{\\left(x-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332963 :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side,  factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+10x+12 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x & =-12 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x & =-12 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+5x) & =-12 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}] & =-12 \\\\ 2[{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4}] & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{25}{4} & =-12 \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{2} & =-12 \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =-12+\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{5}{2}| & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x+\\frac{5}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{6}{3}=-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-3$ and $x=-2.$" "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side,  factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+10x+12 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x & =-12 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x & =-12 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+5x) & =-12 \\\\ 2\\left[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\right] & =-12 \\\\ 2\\left[{\\left(x+\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4}\\right] & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\left(x+\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{25}{4} & =-12 \\\\ 2{\\left(x+\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{25}{2} & =-12 \\\\ 2{\\left(x+\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =-12+\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ 2{\\left(x+\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\left(x+\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {\\left(x+\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{\\left(x+\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{5}{2}| & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x+\\frac{5}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{6}{3}=-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-3$ and $x=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332964 :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-6x-3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-6x & =3 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}x] & =3 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}] & =3 \\\\ 4[{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =3 \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =3 \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =3+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-6x-3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-6x & =3 \\\\ 4\\left[{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}x\\right] & =3 \\\\ 4\\left[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\right] & =3 \\\\ 4\\left[{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}\\right] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =3 \\\\ 4{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =3 \\\\ 4{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2} & =3+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ {\\left(x-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332977 :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨4{t}^{2},\\,-\\frac{3}{32}{t}^{4}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=\\left\\langle 4{t}^{2},\\,-\\frac{3}{32}{t}^{4}\\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332977 :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{32}{t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8t,\\,-\\frac{3}{8}{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{3}{32}{t}^{4}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 8t,\\,-\\frac{3}{8}{t}^{3}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186333103 :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=(4t+\\frac{1}{t}){e}^{t}" "{y}_{p}(t)=\\left(4t+\\frac{1}{t}\\right){e}^{t}"] [:db/cas 17592186333225 :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" "\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186333426 :answer/value "(1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" "\\left(1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186333774 :answer/value "y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=(2-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}" "y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\left(2-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,\\text{m}"] [:db/cas 17592186333794 :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186334003 :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" "\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186334025 :answer/value "(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" "\\left(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186334028 :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=(t-2+\\frac{2}{t}){e}^{t}" "{y}_{p}(t)=\\left(t-2+\\frac{2}{t}\\right){e}^{t}"] [:db/cas 17592186334149 :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186334646 :answer/value "(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" "\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186334676 :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" "\\left(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186334787 :answer/value "y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}" "y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\left(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,\\text{m}"] [:db/cas 17592186335009 :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186335727 :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.$ The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ because this is already included in the complimentary function. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is  \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=t(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.  \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =0,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+(B+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=0,\\,B=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=(1+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =(1+2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.$ The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ because this is already included in the complimentary function. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is  \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=t(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.  \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =0,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+(B+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=0,\\,B=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=(1+2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\left(1+2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\left(1+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335730 :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\pi $ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right),\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩.\n$$" "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\pi $ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left\\langle -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right),\\,\\sqrt{3}\\right\\rangle .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335730 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\pi $:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right),\\,0⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\text{ }\\frac{1}{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\pi $:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left\\langle -\\left(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left\\langle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right),\\,0\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\text{ }\\frac{1}{4}\\left\\langle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,0\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335734 :question/worked-solution "Let $T(t)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the parameter $t.$ Since we are given the curve $r(t)$ parametrized by the parameter $t$ (not the arc length $s$), we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({\\cos }^{2}⁡(3t)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3t))+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the corresponding unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\sqrt{(9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}+(-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\sqrt{{9}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡3t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡3t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\,\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\,}{\\sqrt{10}}=\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $T(t)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the parameter $t.$ Since we are given the curve $r(t)$ parametrized by the parameter $t$ (not the arc length $s$), we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({\\cos }^{2}⁡(3t)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3t))+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the corresponding unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\sqrt{(9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}+(-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\sqrt{{9}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡3t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡3t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\,\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\,}{\\sqrt{10}}=\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335739 :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\left(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\left[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\,j\\right] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335740 :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\right\\rangle \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335740 :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}(-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{8}{25}\\right)}=\\frac{25}{8}.\n$$" "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)\\right),\\,0\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,0\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\left\\langle -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right),\\,0\\right\\rangle  \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)\\right)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{8}{25}\\right)}=\\frac{25}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335742 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{0}⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{(1-{t}^{2})}^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(1-{0}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{0}⟩=⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨0,\\,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(1)^{3}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{0}\\right\\rangle =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{1}{{(1-{t}^{2})}^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{1}{(1-{0}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{0}\\right\\rangle =⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨0,\\,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(1)^{3}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335763 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335764 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\cos ⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\cos ⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335766 :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{\\text{chain rule}}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+6y=12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{2t}+B{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha +6(\\alpha t+\\beta )=12t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha t+(-5\\alpha +6\\beta )=12t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha &=12\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -5\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =\\frac{5}{3},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{\\text{chain rule}}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+6y=12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{2t}+B{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha +6(\\alpha t+\\beta )=12t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha t+(-5\\alpha +6\\beta )=12t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha &=12\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -5\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =\\frac{5}{3},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335768 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$"] [:db/cas 17592186335768 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335772 :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-6w\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{\\csc }^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{\\csc }^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-6w\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{\\csc }^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{\\csc }^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\left(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335773 :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-5x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+8u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u-5{u}^{′}x-10u+8u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}-{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-5x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+8u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u-5{u}^{′}x-10u+8u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}-{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335780 :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}={x}^{2},{f}_{1}=-x,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(2{x}^{3}+4) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})(2{x}^{3}+4) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(2{x}^{3}+4)-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}+4) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2})-x\\cdot 6{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 12x-6{x}^{3} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}-6{x}^{3} \\\\  & =6{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}={x}^{2},{f}_{1}=-x,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(2{x}^{3}+4) \\\\  & =\\left({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)(2{x}^{3}+4) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(2{x}^{3}+4)-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}+4) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2})-x\\cdot 6{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 12x-6{x}^{3} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}-6{x}^{3} \\\\  & =6{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335781 :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients ${a}_{3}=1,{a}_{2}=4,{a}_{1}=4,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({e}^{-2x})+4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{-2x})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(-2{e}^{-2x})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{-2x})+4\\cdot (-2{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-2x})+4\\cdot 4{e}^{-2x}-8{e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-2x}+16{e}^{-2x}-8{e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients ${a}_{3}=1,{a}_{2}=4,{a}_{1}=4,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\right){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({e}^{-2x})+4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{-2x})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(-2{e}^{-2x})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{-2x})+4\\cdot (-2{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-2x})+4\\cdot 4{e}^{-2x}-8{e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-2x}+16{e}^{-2x}-8{e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335800 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)+f(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}(xy))(y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+xy(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}]+[x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)+f(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}(xy))(y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+xy\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\right) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}]+[x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335835 :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335836 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186335836 :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335841 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi }{\\csc }^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi }{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335841 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }{\\csc }^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335843 :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =2 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$" "The difference formula for cosine gives  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =2 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335845 :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f11d5b11c53f7b826291e0b7e95828f57d6f1242b768baf12c874be229b2935.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" "![](images/2f/2f11d5b11c53f7b826291e0b7e95828f57d6f1242b768baf12c874be229b2935.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}+C\\right),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$"] [:db/cas 17592186335845 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))\n$$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/5e/5e9b6568ec493d95db6fe72a6a19024326c5b920629e9ff44a85db54089aa2a6.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,C=\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}+C\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\left(x+\\frac{5C}{3}\\right)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\left(x+\\frac{5C}{3}\\right)\\right)\n$$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/5e/5e9b6568ec493d95db6fe72a6a19024326c5b920629e9ff44a85db54089aa2a6.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,C=\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186335849 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{10})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right).\n$$\n\nRewriting the given expression using the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right).\n$$\n\nRewriting the given expression using the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335854 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in the range of the function." "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in the range of the function."] [:db/cas 17592186335855 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the minimum of the function is $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and it is reached at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the minimum point of the function are $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the minimum of the function is $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and it is reached at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the minimum point of the function are $\\left(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186335856 :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(0,-2\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-2\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-2\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-2\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$" "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(0,-2\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-2\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=-2\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-2\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186335857 :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(0,-\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$" "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(0,-\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=-\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-1\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186335859 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1.$" "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186335860 :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=b\\pi +2n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $b?$" "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=b\\pi +2n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $b?$"] [:db/cas 17592186335868 :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-1\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186335869 :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},-1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},-1\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186335873 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335874 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335874 :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ and are given by the following expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ and are given by the following expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335875 :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2.$" "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186335875 :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-1$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2\\ge 1+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-1$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2\\le -1+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 3,\\,f(x)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$" "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-1$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+2\\ge 1+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-1$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+2\\le -1+2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 3,\\,f(x)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1]\\cup [3,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186335877 :question/problem "Find the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.\n$$" "Find the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335877 :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\le -\\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\ge \\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).\n$$" "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\le -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-1\\le -\\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-1\\ge \\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335878 :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "What is the domain of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335878 :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $$\n\\displaystyle -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{6}+3n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+3n\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer." "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $$\n\\displaystyle -\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{6}+3n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+3n\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335880 :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "What is the domain of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-1?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335880 :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1,\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-1,\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $\\left(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335881 :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4$ at its local minimum points?" "What is the value of $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+4$ at its local minimum points?"] [:db/cas 17592186335881 :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and adding $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 2 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4\\ge 2+4 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4\\ge 6 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=6$ at its local minimum points." "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and adding $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\le -1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\ge 2 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+4\\ge 2+4 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+4\\ge 6 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=6$ at its local minimum points."] [:db/cas 17592186335943 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{3{x}^{4}+2}{{x}^{3}-7x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{7x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+0)}{(1-0+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{3{x}^{4}+2}{{x}^{3}-7x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{7x}{{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{\\left(3x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+0)}{(1-0+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335944 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{2{x}^{2}+x+4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{x})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{2{x}^{2}+x+4} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{\\left(2+\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335945 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{4{x}^{5}-9}{{x}^{3}+8x} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(4{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(1+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{4{x}^{2}-0}{1+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{4{x}^{5}-9}{{x}^{3}+8x} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8x}{{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{\\left(4{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(1+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{4{x}^{2}-0}{1+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335946 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}+6}{5x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{5x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x})}{5} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+0)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}+6}{5x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{x}+\\frac{6}{x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{5x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x}\\right)}{5} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+0)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186335947 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-7{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-7{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(2-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{2}+0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{-7{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(-7{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(2-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{2}+0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186335948 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5+7{x}^{3}}{12x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{12x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5}{x}+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(0+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5+7{x}^{3}}{12x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{12x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{x}+7{x}^{2}\\right)}{12} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(0+7{x}^{2})}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186335951 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+3) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}+3) \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+3) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}+3\\right) \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335957 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right)=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {[\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right)=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {\\left[\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}\\right]}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335958 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\frac{x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\frac{x}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-1\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left[\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,1\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335975 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}+6x+1}{1-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4+\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4+\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4+0+0}{0-2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is  $y=-2.$" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}+6x+1}{1-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(4+\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{\\left(4+\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2\\right)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4+0+0}{0-2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is  $y=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186335976 :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{7\\,\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}=7\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $7x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=7x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}7\\int \\frac{1}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =7\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsec} (7x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{7\\,\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}=7\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $7x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=7x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}7\\int \\frac{1}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =7\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsec} (7x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335977 :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}=3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $9x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=9x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec} (9x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}=3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\operatorname{arcsec} ,$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} (u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $9x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=9x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsec} u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec} (9x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186336001 :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{x}^{2}-27x-70}{(2x+3)(4-x)(x+2)}=\\frac{A}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{B}{(4-x)}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(2x+3)(4-x)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-27x-70=A(4-x)(x+2)+B(2x+3)(x+2)+C(2x+3)(4-x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-27\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-70 & =A(4-\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right))(\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+2) \\\\  & \\,+B(2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+3)(\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+2) \\\\  & \\,+C(2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+3)(4-\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)) \\\\ \\frac{63}{4}+\\frac{81}{2}-70 & =\\frac{11}{4}A \\\\ -\\frac{55}{4} & =\\frac{11}{4}A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{55}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{11} \\\\ A & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=4$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(4)^{2}-27(4)-70 & =A(4-4)(4+2) \\\\  & \\,+B(2(4)+3)(4+2) \\\\  & \\,+C(2(4)+3)(4-4) \\\\ 112-108-70 & =B(11)(6) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-66}{66} \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-2)^{2}-27(-2)-70 & =A(4-(-2))((-2)+2) \\\\  & \\,+B(2(-2)+3)((-2)+2) \\\\  & \\,+C(2(-2)+3)(4-(-2)) \\\\ 28+54-70 & =C(-1)(6) \\\\ C & =\\frac{12}{-6} \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B+C=-5+(-1)+(-2)=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{x}^{2}-27x-70}{(2x+3)(4-x)(x+2)}=\\frac{A}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{B}{(4-x)}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(2x+3)(4-x)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-27x-70=A(4-x)(x+2)+B(2x+3)(x+2)+C(2x+3)(4-x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-27\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-70 & =A\\left(4-\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\left(\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+2\\right) \\\\  & \\,+B\\left(2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+3\\right)\\left(\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+2\\right) \\\\  & \\,+C\\left(2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+3\\right)\\left(4-\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ \\frac{63}{4}+\\frac{81}{2}-70 & =\\frac{11}{4}A \\\\ -\\frac{55}{4} & =\\frac{11}{4}A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{55}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{11} \\\\ A & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=4$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(4)^{2}-27(4)-70 & =A(4-4)(4+2) \\\\  & \\,+B(2(4)+3)(4+2) \\\\  & \\,+C(2(4)+3)(4-4) \\\\ 112-108-70 & =B(11)(6) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-66}{66} \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-2)^{2}-27(-2)-70 & =A(4-(-2))((-2)+2) \\\\  & \\,+B(2(-2)+3)((-2)+2) \\\\  & \\,+C(2(-2)+3)(4-(-2)) \\\\ 28+54-70 & =C(-1)(6) \\\\ C & =\\frac{12}{-6} \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B+C=-5+(-1)+(-2)=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186336006 :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+29x+1}{(4x-1)(x+3)(x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{(4x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(4x-1)(x+3)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}+29x+1=A(x+3)(x+1)+B(4x-1)(x+1)+C(4x-1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{1}{4}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}+29\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+1 & =A(\\frac{1}{4}+3)(\\frac{1}{4}+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(4\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1)(\\frac{1}{4}+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(4\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1)(\\frac{1}{4}+3) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{29}{4}+1 & =A\\left(\\frac{65}{16}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{65}{8} & =A\\left(\\frac{65}{16}\\right) \\\\ A & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(-3)^{2}+29(-3)+1 & =A(-3+3)(-3+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(4(-3)-1)(-3+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(4(-3)-1)(-3+3) \\\\ -18-87+1 & =B(-13)(-2) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-104}{26} \\\\ B & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-1$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(-1)^{2}+29(-1)+1 & =A(-1+3)(-1+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(4(-1)-1)(-1+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(4(-1)-1)(-1+3) \\\\ -2-29+1 & =C(-5)(2) \\\\ C & =\\frac{-30}{-10} \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+29x+1}{(4x-1)(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{2}{(4x-1)}-\\frac{4}{(x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x+1)}.\n$$" "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+29x+1}{(4x-1)(x+3)(x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{(4x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(4x-1)(x+3)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}+29x+1=A(x+3)(x+1)+B(4x-1)(x+1)+C(4x-1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{1}{4}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}+29\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+1 & =A\\left(\\frac{1}{4}+3\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{4}+1\\right) \\\\  & \\,+B\\left(4\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{4}+1\\right) \\\\  & \\,+C\\left(4\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-1\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{4}+3\\right) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{29}{4}+1 & =A\\left(\\frac{65}{16}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{65}{8} & =A\\left(\\frac{65}{16}\\right) \\\\ A & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(-3)^{2}+29(-3)+1 & =A(-3+3)(-3+1) \\\\  &