 and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right):$\n \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338733 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8-3{x}^{3}}{1-16{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{16{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-16)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}},\\frac{3}{x}$ and  $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$  become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-16)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{0-16} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8-3{x}^{3}}{1-16{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{16{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-16\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}},\\frac{3}{x}$ and  $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$  become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-16\\right)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{0-16} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186338740 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{6-3x+4{x}^{2}}{12x-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{12x}{x}-\\frac{21}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-3+4x)}{(12-\\frac{21}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{21}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-3+4x)}{(12-\\frac{21}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0-3+4x}{12-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x-3}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{6-3x+4{x}^{2}}{12x-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{12x}{x}-\\frac{21}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{6}{x}-3+4x\\right)}{\\left(12-\\frac{21}{x}\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{21}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{6}{x}-3+4x\\right)}{\\left(12-\\frac{21}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0-3+4x}{12-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x-3}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186338786 :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,v\\,| & =\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+0+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $v$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot ⟨-4,0,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5},0,\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,v\\,| & =\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+0+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $v$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot ⟨-4,0,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{4}{5},0,\\frac{3}{5}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338792 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its zero occurs at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)" "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its zero occurs at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186338793 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its zero occurs at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)" "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its zero occurs at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186338805 :question/problem "Differentiate $\\mathbf{f}(t)+4\\mathbf{g}(t)$ with respect to $t$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Differentiate $\\mathbf{f}(t)+4\\mathbf{g}(t)$ with respect to $t$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =\\left\\langle t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{4}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338805 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+4\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+4{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}4{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+4\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+4{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩+4⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩+⟨4,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}16{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+4,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}0+16{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1+8\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{t-4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}16{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+4\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+4{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}4{t}^{3}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+4\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+4{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0\\right\\rangle +4\\left\\langle 1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}4{t}^{3}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0\\right\\rangle +\\left\\langle 4,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}16{t}^{3}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+4,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}0+16{t}^{3}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1+8\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{t-4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}16{t}^{3}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338809 :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$" "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{