:question/worked-solution "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. Therefore, we can calculate it using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5-1}{2},\\frac{3-3}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{2},\\frac{0}{2}) \\\\  & =(2,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. Therefore, we can calculate it using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =\\left(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{5-1}{2},\\frac{3-3}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{4}{2},\\frac{0}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =(2,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338864 :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $C(1,\\sqrt{2},-1)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}=⟨0,11,\\frac{1}{3}⟩+s⟨1,2,-3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨0,\\sqrt{2},5⟩+t⟨-1,0,3⟩.$" "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $C(1,\\sqrt{2},-1)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}=\\left\\langle 0,11,\\frac{1}{3}\\right\\rangle +s⟨1,2,-3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨0,\\sqrt{2},5⟩+t⟨-1,0,3⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186338865 :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $D(6,2,1)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,9⟩+s⟨4,-1,5⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨\\sqrt{3},10,-1⟩+t⟨0,2,3⟩.$" "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $D(6,2,1)$ and is parallel to the lines $\\mathbf{r}=\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},1,9\\right\\rangle +s⟨4,-1,5⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨\\sqrt{3},10,-1⟩+t⟨0,2,3⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186338867 :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is  \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(7,3)$ and $Y(-1,3),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{{(7-(-1))}^{2}+{(3-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{7+(-1)}{2},\\frac{3+3}{2})=(3,3)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=4$ and center $(a,b)=(3,3),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is  \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(7,3)$ and $Y(-1,3),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{{(7-(-1))}^{2}+{(3-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7+(-1)}{2},\\frac{3+3}{2}\\right)=(3,3)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=4$ and center $(a,b)=(3,3),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338868 :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is  \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-3,2)$ and $Y(3,4),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{{(-3-3)}^{2}+{(2-4)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{2}=\\sqrt{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{-3+3}{2},\\frac{2+4}{2})=(0,3)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{10}$ and center $(a,b)=(0,3),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{10})}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is  \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-3,2)$ and $Y(3,4),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{{(-3-3)}^{2}+{(2-4)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{2}=\\sqrt{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{-3+3}{2},\\frac{2+4}{2}\\right)=(0,3)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{10}$ and center $(a,b)=(0,3),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{10})}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338903 :question/worked-solution "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =\\frac{11}{36} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =\\frac{121}{36} \\\\ z & =\\pm \\frac{11}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-\\frac{11}{6}$ and $z=\\frac{11}{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-\\frac{11}{6})$ and $(0,0,\\frac{11}{6}).$" "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =\\frac{11}{36} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =\\frac{121}{36} \\\\ z & =\\pm \\frac{11}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-\\frac{11}{6}$ and $z=\\frac{11}{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $\\left(0,0,-\\frac{11}{6}\\right)$ and $\\left(0,0,\\frac{11}{6}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186338922 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{x(y-x)}{2x(y+1)}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-1.$ Given that the point $P\\left(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{x(y-x)}{2x(y+1)}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186338922 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,0) & =\\frac{(-1)(0-(-1))}{2(-1)(0+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,0) & ={f}_{y}(-1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $y-2z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,2,1⟩.$" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,0) & =\\frac{(-1)(0-(-1))}{2(-1)(0+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,0) & ={f}_{y}(-1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P\\left(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $y-2z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,2,1⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186338929 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186338929 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, can be found by computing $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }i+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})‖} & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$, can be found by computing $∇f\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }i+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)‖} & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338930 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x+3y-2xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1).$" "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x+3y-2xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{3}{2},1\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186338930 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(1-2y)\\text{ }i+(3-2x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1) & =(1-2(1))\\text{ }i+(3-2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(\\frac{3}{2},1)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(\\frac{3}{2},1)‖} & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}}{1}=-\\mathbf{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{3}{2},1\\right)$, can be found by computing $∇f\\left(\\frac{3}{2},1\\right).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{3}{2},1\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(1-2y)\\text{ }i+(3-2x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f\\left(\\frac{3}{2},1\\right) & =(1-2(1))\\text{ }i+\\left(3-2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f\\left(\\frac{3}{2},1\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f\\left(\\frac{3}{2},1\\right)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f\\left(\\frac{3}{2},1\\right)‖} & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}}{1}=-\\mathbf{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338933 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-1} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ 1-x & =-y \\\\ x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2\\right)\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-1\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-2\\right)\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-1\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-1} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ 1-x & =-y \\\\ x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338936 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =2xy \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2y)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(0))\\,j \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,1)=⟨-1,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1} & =\\frac{y-1}{1} \\\\ -x & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =2xy \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2y\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2x\\right)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(1)\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(0)\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,1)=⟨-1,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1} & =\\frac{y-1}{1} \\\\ -x & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338939 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{z}^{2}-xy}\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}-xy}-z)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-xy}-y)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-0)\\,j+(\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-(-2))\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{z}^{2}-xy}\\text{ }i+\\left(-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}-xy}-z\\right)\\text{ }j+\\left(\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-xy}-y\\right)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-0\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-(-2)\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186338952 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)+f(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}((2xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+2xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2xy+{y}^{2}}{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+(-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x+2y}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+(-\\frac{x+y}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{x+y}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{x+y}{x}+\\frac{2x+y}{x})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(-\\frac{x+y}{y}+\\frac{x+2y}{y})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)+f(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}((2xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+2xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2xy+{y}^{2}}{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x+2y}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{x+y}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{x+y}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{x+y}{x}+\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\right)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\left(-\\frac{x+y}{y}+\\frac{x+2y}{y}\\right)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338956 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+9)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=e,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(e) & =⟨2e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+e,4e⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3e,4e⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=e$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(e),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(e)| & =\\sqrt{(3e)^{2}+(4e)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+9)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=e,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(e) & =⟨2e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+e,4e⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3e,4e⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=e$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(e),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(e)| & =\\sqrt{(3e)^{2}+(4e)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338967 :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-21),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}+3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{t}^{2},\\,8{t}^{3}+3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨24t,\\,24{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-21),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}+3t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨12{t}^{2},\\,8{t}^{3}+3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}+3)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨24t,\\,24{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338982 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\right)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338982 :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\right)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338983 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (8x-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\left(8x-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\right)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338983 :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (8x-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 8x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(8x-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\right)\\text{d}x & =\\int 8x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338984 :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (5{x}^{4}+\\frac{7}{1+{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{7}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C \\\\  & ={x}^{5}+7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(5{x}^{4}+\\frac{7}{1+{x}^{2}}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{7}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C \\\\  & ={x}^{5}+7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338985 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{4}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\left(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{4}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\right)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338985 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{4}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{4}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\right)\\text{d}x & =\\int 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338987 :question/worked-solution "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)+1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)+1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339004 :question/worked-solution "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1 & =\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{5} \\\\ (z-1)^{2} & =5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1) \\\\ (z-1) & =\\pm \\sqrt{5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}=1$, including its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$" "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1 & =\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{5} \\\\ (z-1)^{2} & =5\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1\\right) \\\\ (z-1) & =\\pm \\sqrt{5\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1\\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}=1$, including its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339068 :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨0,5⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 0\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 5\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,5t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨1,\\,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,5(2)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,10+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=-10.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨1,\\,5t-10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,\\,5t-10⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (5t-10)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+{C}_{3},\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(2)=⟨3,-3⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2+{C}_{3},\\,\\frac{5(2)^{2}}{2}-10(2)+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-3⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{3},\\,-10+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=1$ and ${C}_{4}=7.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨t+1,\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨0,5⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 0\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 5\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,5t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨1,\\,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,5(2)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,10+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=-10.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨1,\\,5t-10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,\\,5t-10⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (5t-10)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle t+{C}_{3},\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+{C}_{4}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(2)=⟨3,-3⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =\\left\\langle 2+{C}_{3},\\,\\frac{5(2)^{2}}{2}-10(2)+{C}_{4}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ ⟨3,-3⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{3},\\,-10+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=1$ and ${C}_{4}=7.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\left\\langle t+1,\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+7\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339073 :question/worked-solution "First, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\left(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339074 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}=2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ [x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)]+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}) & =6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}=2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\left[x\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\right]+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}) & =6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339075 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x{e}^{y}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ [(x)({e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+(1)({e}^{y})] & =2x \\\\ x\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x \\\\ x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-{e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x{e}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x{e}^{y}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\left[(x)\\left({e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+(1)({e}^{y})\\right] & =2x \\\\ x\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x \\\\ x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-{e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x{e}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339079 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+4y & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y+4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ [{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x]+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+4) & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+4y & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y+4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\left[{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x\\right]+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+4) & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339080 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $2xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+4{y}^{3} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2xy+4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\ [2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y]+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2x+12{y}^{2}) & =2x-2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-2y}{2x+12{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{x+6{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $2xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+4{y}^{3} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2xy+4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\left[2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\right]+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2x+12{y}^{2}) & =2x-2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-2y}{2x+12{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{x+6{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339081 :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $2{x}^{3}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6) \\\\ [2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 6{x}^{2}]+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6{x}^{2}y+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x-6{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}+4y) & =8x-6{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8x-6{x}^{2}y}{2{x}^{3}+4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $2{x}^{3}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6) \\\\ \\left[2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 6{x}^{2}\\right]+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6{x}^{2}y+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x-6{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}+4y) & =8x-6{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8x-6{x}^{2}y}{2{x}^{3}+4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339084 :question/worked-solution "First, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =-2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\right)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =-2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339091 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}}{\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}} \\\\  & =4\\lim_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{\\left[\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}}{\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}} \\\\  & =4\\lim_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339092 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)}.\n$$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1\\right)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339092 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1\\right)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1\\right)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{\\left[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right]}^{′}}{{\\left[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1\\right]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339093 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)}=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8\\right)}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339093 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6{x}^{-7}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8\\right)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8\\right)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{\\left[\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right]}^{′}}{{\\left[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8\\right]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6{x}^{-7}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339096 :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius is equal to ${F}^{′}(C).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $F(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{9}{5}C+32) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius is $\\frac{9}{5}$ degrees Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius." "The instantaneous rate of change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius is equal to ${F}^{′}(C).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $F(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}\\left(\\frac{9}{5}C+32\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius is $\\frac{9}{5}$ degrees Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius."] [:db/cas 17592186339105 :question/worked-solution "First, we write out the inner summation by summing over the index $i:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}\\frac{i}{j} & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}\\frac{i}{j}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}(\\frac{1}{j}+\\frac{2}{j}+\\frac{3}{j}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}\\frac{6}{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we sum over the index $j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}\\frac{6}{j} & =\\frac{6}{1}+\\frac{6}{2}+\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =6+3+2 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write out the inner summation by summing over the index $i:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}\\frac{i}{j} & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}\\left[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}\\frac{i}{j}\\right] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{j}+\\frac{2}{j}+\\frac{3}{j}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}\\frac{6}{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we sum over the index $j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}\\frac{6}{j} & =\\frac{6}{1}+\\frac{6}{2}+\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =6+3+2 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339111 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int -15(5x-7)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x-7.$Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -15(5x-7)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -15{u}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\int {u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right){u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x-7)^{-3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (5x-7)^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-7)+0 \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (5x-7)^{-4}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{(5x-7)^{4}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int -15(5x-7)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x-7.$Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -15(5x-7)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -15{u}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\int {u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right){u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C\\right] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}\\right]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x-7)^{-3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (5x-7)^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-7)+0 \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (5x-7)^{-4}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{(5x-7)^{4}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339113 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 4(8-x)^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=8-x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4(8-x)^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int {u}^{-5}\\cdot (-1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)\\int {u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\int {u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{(-4)}{u}^{-4}+C \\\\  & =(8-x)^{-4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(8-x)^{-4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =(-4)(8-x)^{-5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-x)+0 \\\\  & =(-4)(8-x)^{-5}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4(8-x)^{-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(8-x)^{5}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 4(8-x)^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=8-x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4(8-x)^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int {u}^{-5}\\cdot (-1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)\\int {u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\int {u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{(-4)}{u}^{-4}+C \\\\  & =(8-x)^{-4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}+C\\right] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[\\frac{1}{(8-x)^{4}}\\right]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(8-x)^{-4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =(-4)(8-x)^{-5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-x)+0 \\\\  & =(-4)(8-x)^{-5}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4(8-x)^{-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(8-x)^{5}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339118 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{1}{4x-5}+\\frac{1}{1-4x})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\left(\\frac{1}{4x-5}+\\frac{1}{1-4x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186339118 :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{4x-5}+\\frac{1}{1-4x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-5|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{4x-5}{1-4x}|+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{4x-5}{1-4x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm." "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{4x-5}+\\frac{1}{1-4x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-5|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{4x-5}{1-4x}|+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(K|\\frac{4x-5}{1-4x}|\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."] [:db/cas 17592186339119 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\left(\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2x-1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant."] [:db/cas 17592186339119 :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(2x+1)(2x-1)|+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(2x+1)(2x-1)|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm." "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2x-1}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(2x+1)(2x-1)|+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(2x+1)(2x-1)|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."] [:db/cas 17592186339120 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{1}{5x+2}+\\frac{1}{5x-2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\left(\\frac{1}{5x+2}+\\frac{1}{5x-2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive const