$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/97/9752e135eee9c4e585a007f84b0b8b8d220d4e2e1f027fc8a69aebf27066940e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $\\left[0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348171 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/f8/f8617da98ea9d9fb48b8fef588b624c6961a299233836fe433c1819b697ca0fb.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $$\n\\displaystyle 0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(3\\left(x+\\frac{C}{3}\\right)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(3\\left(x+\\frac{C}{3}\\right)\\right)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/f8/f8617da98ea9d9fb48b8fef588b624c6961a299233836fe433c1819b697ca0fb.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $$\n\\displaystyle 0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348177 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-4).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](images/b7/b71fec9561c150627ca6cfce06e8e79a9fa77f9395930f4bb9cf1a755cf350b2.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-4).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](images/b7/b71fec9561c150627ca6cfce06e8e79a9fa77f9395930f4bb9cf1a755cf350b2.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $\\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348186 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/83/8349b65a4c7f8df5621e79441a2110c499ebfbe89238ba744f73885a4a185b6b.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/83/8349b65a4c7f8df5621e79441a2110c499ebfbe89238ba744f73885a4a185b6b.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $\\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348187 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=1.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/dd/ddd66398b45c22772af257d6856454b63fde930253090329f99383e4e063fff4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=1.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/dd/ddd66398b45c22772af257d6856454b63fde930253090329f99383e4e063fff4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $\\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348190 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/30/30bb7c769ff0cbabcb13e3f48aaad1e4c8c34ded6b571858248eb9819eb2bbe4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/30/30bb7c769ff0cbabcb13e3f48aaad1e4c8c34ded6b571858248eb9819eb2bbe4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $\\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348228 :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(\\frac{3}{2},-1)$, which lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$, which lies in the first quadrant.\n\n![](images/d7/d7390ddcc8d338d2fd46bc12b0a4c65f8e6bf6e8c4f6c9fa802e2acea159416b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$ only." "- The complex number $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $\\left(\\frac{3}{2},-1\\right)$, which lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$, which lies in the first quadrant.\n\n![](images/d7/d7390ddcc8d338d2fd46bc12b0a4c65f8e6bf6e8c4f6c9fa802e2acea159416b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186348235 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{2}{5}\\,{x}^{5/2})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\,{x}^{5/2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\,{x}^{5/2}\\right)+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\,{x}^{5/2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348245 :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-2t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a linear polynomial. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a linear polynomial, i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha +\\beta t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,$ $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2-2t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+2-2t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at $y=2\\,\\text{m}$ with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=2$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2-2t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{-2(\\pi /4)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+2-2\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-2t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a linear polynomial. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a linear polynomial, i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha +\\beta t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,$ $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2-2t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)+2-2t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at $y=2\\,\\text{m}$ with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=2$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2-2t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{-2(\\pi /4)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+2-2\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348247 :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=-1,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=-1,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348253 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}{y}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2{y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2{y}^{1/2}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C \\\\ {y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ y & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{C}{2})}^{2} \\\\ y & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}+K)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\frac{C}{2}.$\n\nUse this family on a connected interval where $\\frac{x^{3/2}}3+K>0$. This condition preserves the positive square-root branch used before raising both sides to a power. The calculation above works in the stated positive quadrant; $\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{y}$ is valid there." "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}{y}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2{y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2{y}^{1/2}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C \\\\ {y}^{1/2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ y & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{C}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ y & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}+K\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\frac{C}{2}.$\n\nUse this family on a connected interval where $\\frac{x^{3/2}}3+K>0$. This condition preserves the positive square-root branch used before raising both sides to a power. The calculation above works in the stated positive quadrant; $\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{y}$ is valid there."] [:db/cas 17592186348261 :question/worked-solution "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ -y({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y{e}^{x}+{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+\\underbrace{(-5{e}^{x})}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{-{e}^{x}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5{e}^{x}y & =-{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-5{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x)=-{e}^{x}.$" "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ -y\\left({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1\\right) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}\\left({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y{e}^{x}+{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+\\underbrace{(-5{e}^{x})}_{P(x)}y & =\\underbrace{-{e}^{x}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5{e}^{x}y & =-{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-5{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x)=-{e}^{x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348283 :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y={(z+{x}^{2})}^{1/2}\\,.\n$$\n\n]\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2}}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x).\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2}}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x)+(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2}} \\\\ x(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x)+2(z+{x}^{2}) & =8{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x+\\frac{2}{x}(z+{x}^{2}) & =8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=4x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/x\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\,z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 4x \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =4{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & ={x}^{4}+C \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $y>0.$" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y={(z+{x}^{2})}^{1/2}\\,.\n$$\n\n]\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2}}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2}}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x\\right)+(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(z+{x}^{2})^{1/2}} \\\\ x\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x\\right)+2(z+{x}^{2}) & =8{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2x+\\frac{2}{x}(z+{x}^{2}) & =8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=4x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/x\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\,z\\right) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 4x \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =4{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & ={x}^{4}+C \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $y>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348286 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi })) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{8}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\pi -0)+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2(-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi -16}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(4\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(4\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(4\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{8}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\pi -0)+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2(-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi -16}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348287 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(1.5,1.5).$" "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $\\left(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ to $(1.5,1.5).$"] [:db/cas 17592186348287 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(1.5,1.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.5-2=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =1.5-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+0\\cdot (1.5-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ≈-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $0.5.$" "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $\\left(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f\\left(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $\\left(2+Δx,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δy\\right),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $\\left(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ to $(1.5,1.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.5-2=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =1.5-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+0\\cdot \\left(1.5-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & ≈-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $0.5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348289 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2xy\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,4) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x+({0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =-1-0=-1, \\\\ Δy & =5-4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.25.$" "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2xy\\,\\text{d}x+\\left({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,4) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x+\\left({0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =-1-0=-1, \\\\ Δy & =5-4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.25.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348293 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1).$" "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $\\left(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ to $(0,1,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186348293 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,\\text{d}y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,\\text{d}x+(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))\\,\\text{d}y-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,0+Δy,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-1=-1, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1 \\\\ Δz & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $1.$" "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,\\text{d}y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $\\left(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f\\left(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\text{d}y-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $\\left(1+Δx,0+Δy,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δz\\right),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $\\left(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ to $(0,1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-1=-1, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1 \\\\ Δz & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot \\left(1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & ≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348294 :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) & =x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx) & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y\\right) & =x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx) & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348295 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y\\right) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348300 :question/problem "Estimate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0).$" "Estimate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ at the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186348300 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)=f(1,0.12),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1,0.12).$\n\nMoving from $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0)$ to $(1,0.12),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{3}, \\\\ Δy & =0.12-0=0.12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0.12) \\\\  & ≈0+0.5\\cdot 0.12 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.06.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0.12) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0)+0.06 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12) & ≈\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x+\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0\\right),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+Δx,0+Δy\\right),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)=f(1,0.12),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1,0.12).$\n\nMoving from $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0\\right)$ to $(1,0.12),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{3}, \\\\ Δy & =0.12-0=0.12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot \\left(1-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0.12) \\\\  & ≈0+0.5\\cdot 0.12 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.06.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0.12) & ≈f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0\\right)+0.06 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12) & ≈\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348305 :question/worked-solution "The substitution $z={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2z=6.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=2,\\,Q(x)=6\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{2x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2z) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 6 \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}z & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x}z) & =6{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{2x}z & =3{e}^{2x}+C \\\\ z & =3+C{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{2} & =3+C{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{3+C{e}^{-2x}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+C{e}^{-2x}}}{x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $x>0$ and $y>0.$\n\nFrom the initial condition $y(1)=\\sqrt{3}$ we determine $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+C{e}^{-2}}}{1}\\,⟹\\,C=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The substitution $z={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2z=6.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=2,\\,Q(x)=6\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{2x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2z\\right) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 6 \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}z & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x}z) & =6{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{2x}z & =3{e}^{2x}+C \\\\ z & =3+C{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{2} & =3+C{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{3+C{e}^{-2x}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+C{e}^{-2x}}}{x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $x>0$ and $y>0.$\n\nFrom the initial condition $y(1)=\\sqrt{3}$ we determine $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3+C{e}^{-2}}}{1}\\,⟹\\,C=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348314 :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{4}{e}^{x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{4}{e}^{x/2})-4({u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{2}{e}^{x/2})+u{e}^{x/2} & =0 \\\\ 4{u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+u{e}^{x/2}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}-2u{e}^{x/2}+u{e}^{x/2} & =0 \\\\ 4{u}^{″}{e}^{x/2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4{e}^{x/2}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x/2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x/2}({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x/2}\\cdot x=x{e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}.\n$$" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{4}{e}^{x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\left({u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{4}{e}^{x/2}\\right)-4\\left({u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{2}{e}^{x/2}\\right)+u{e}^{x/2} & =0 \\\\ 4{u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+u{e}^{x/2}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}-2u{e}^{x/2}+u{e}^{x/2} & =0 \\\\ 4{u}^{″}{e}^{x/2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4{e}^{x/2}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x/2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x/2}({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x/2}\\cdot x=x{e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348315 :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-2t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+2(u{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}+2u{t}^{2}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{3}-4u{t}^{2}+2u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}(t{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nt{w}^{′}+2w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}){t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-{t}^{2}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{2}+Bt.\n$$" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-2t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+2(u{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{3}+2u{t}^{2}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{3}-4u{t}^{2}+2u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{4}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}(t{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nt{w}^{′}+2w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=\\left(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}\\right){t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-{t}^{2}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{2}+Bt.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348316 :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-3x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+2(u{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{4}+8{u}^{′}{x}^{3}+4u{x}^{2}-3{u}^{′}{x}^{3}-6u{x}^{2}+2u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{4}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2x{w}^{′}+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5/2}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5/2}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=(-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+{c}_{2}){x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({x}^{-3/2}){x}^{2}={x}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{1/2}.\n$$" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-3x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+2(u{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{4}+8{u}^{′}{x}^{3}+4u{x}^{2}-3{u}^{′}{x}^{3}-6u{x}^{2}+2u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{4}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2x{w}^{′}+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5/2}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5/2}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\left(-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+{c}_{2}\\right){x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({x}^{-3/2}){x}^{2}={x}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{1/2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348345 :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =1+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =4 \\\\ R & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$" "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =1+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =4 \\\\ R & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348387 :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨3{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" "If ${F}^{′}(t)=\\left\\langle 3{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}\\right\\rangle ,$ then $F(t)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186348387 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int (3{t}^{2}-5)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\left\\langle 3{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}\\right\\rangle \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left\\langle 3{t}^{2}-5,\\,\\frac{1}{t}\\right\\rangle \\text{d}t & =\\left\\langle \\int (3{t}^{2}-5)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-5t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration."] [:db/cas 17592186348401 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ over the rectangle $R=[1,e]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ over the rectangle $R=[1,e]\\times \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right].$"] [:db/cas 17592186348401 :question/worked-solution "We apply the multiplicative property of double integrals and then evaluate each of the integrals separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{1}^{e}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the multiplicative property of double integrals and then evaluate each of the integrals separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\left({\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\right)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{1}^{e}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot \\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\right) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348403 :question/worked-solution "We apply the multiplicative property of double integrals and then evaluate each of the integrals separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{{e}^{y}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\right)({e}^{y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot [{e}^{y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1)\\cdot ({e}^{1}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the multiplicative property of double integrals and then evaluate each of the integrals separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{{e}^{y}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\right)({e}^{y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\left({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\right)({\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x]_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot [{e}^{y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1)\\cdot ({e}^{1}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348404 :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right),\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)⟩,\\,0\\le s<10\\pi .\n$$" "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=\\left\\langle 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right),\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\right\\rangle ,\\,0\\le s<10\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348404 :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\left\\langle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\sqrt{{\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348411 :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s,$ where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\frac{1}{3}(1-2s)^{3/2},\\,\\frac{1}{3}(2s)^{3/2}⟩,\\,0<s<\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s,$ where\n\n$$\nr(s)=\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{3}(1-2s)^{3/2},\\,\\frac{1}{3}(2s)^{3/2}\\right\\rangle ,\\,0<s<\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348411 :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{3}(1-2s)^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{3}(2s)^{3/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (1-2s)^{1/2}\\cdot (-2),\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (2s)^{1/2}\\cdot 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-(1-2s)^{1/2},\\,(2s)^{1/2}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-(1-2s)^{1/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}((2s)^{1/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-2s)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-2),\\,\\frac{1}{2}(2s)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(1-2s)^{-1/2},\\,(2s)^{-1/2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the curvature is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{((1-2s)^{-1/2})}^{2}+{((2s)^{-1/2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-2s)^{-1}+(2s)^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1-2s}+\\frac{1}{2s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2s(1-2s)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(1-2s)^{3/2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(2s)^{3/2}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (1-2s)^{1/2}\\cdot (-2),\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (2s)^{1/2}\\cdot 2\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-(1-2s)^{1/2},\\,(2s)^{1/2}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-(1-2s)^{1/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}((2s)^{1/2})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-2s)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-2),\\,\\frac{1}{2}(2s)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨(1-2s)^{-1/2},\\,(2s)^{-1/2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the curvature is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{((1-2s)^{-1/2})}^{2}+{((2s)^{-1/2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-2s)^{-1}+(2s)^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1-2s}+\\frac{1}{2s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2s(1-2s)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348420 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}⟨1,s⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}\\right),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{s}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{1}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-s,1⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1+{1}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}‖⟨-1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}⟨1,s⟩\\right) \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}\\right),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{1+{s}^{2}}}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{s}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{1}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1+{s}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-s,1⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1+{1}^{2})^{3/2}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}‖⟨-1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348424 :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}6y\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =(4-2){\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{x}{|}_{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{1}\\right)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}6y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(2-1){\\int }_{2}^{4}6y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =3({4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=2+36=38.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+6y\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{R}6y\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =(4-2){\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{x}{|}_{1}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{2}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{1}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}6y\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(2-1){\\int }_{2}^{4}6y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =3({4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{2+6y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=2+36=38.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348429 :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣x>0},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2}{x}-4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{x}-4y & -4x+2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-4y &=0 \\\\ -4x+2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{x}-4y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ -4x+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{1}{x}. \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=1 \\\\ x=-\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1).$ We reject the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$" "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣x>0},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2}{x}-4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{x}-4y & -4x+2y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-4y &=0 \\\\ -4x+2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{x}-4y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ -4x+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{1}{x}. \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=1 \\\\ x=-\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},1\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},-1\\right).$ We reject the point $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},-1\\right)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},1\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186348432 :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}\\frac{f(x,y)}{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the linearity and additivity of double integrals, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C\\cup D}\\frac{f(x,y)}{2}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{C\\cup D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({∬}_{C}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348435 :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3{x}^{2}+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-6xy & -3{x}^{2}+12y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6xy &=0 \\\\ -3{x}^{2}+12y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+12y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-\\frac{x}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}=0 \\\\ x=2 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3{x}^{2}+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-6xy & -3{x}^{2}+12y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6xy &=0 \\\\ -3{x}^{2}+12y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+12y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\left(1-\\frac{x}{2}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}=0 \\\\ x=2 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points."] [:db/cas 17592186348444 :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-y)=4xy-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2x)=4xy-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2})=x{e}^{x}+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1){e}^{x}+2xy)=x{e}^{x}+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“${f}_{x}(x,y)=yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=(x-1){e}^{x}+2xy$” and “${f}_{x}(x,y)=x-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x$” only'." "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-y)=4xy-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2x)=4xy-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2})=x{e}^{x}+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1){e}^{x}+2xy)=x{e}^{x}+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“${f}_{x}(x,y)=yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=(x-1){e}^{x}+2xy$” and “${f}_{x}(x,y)=x-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x$” only'."] [:db/cas 17592186348528 :answer/value "1-{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}" "1-{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186348534 :answer/value "(-\\frac{11}{2},-4\\sqrt{2})" "\\left(-\\frac{11}{2},-4\\sqrt{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186348582 :answer/value "1+4{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}" "1+4{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186348614 :answer/value "\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }({\\int }_{-2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)" "\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\left({\\int }_{-2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186348620 :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186348768 :answer/value "\\lim_{b\\to (-3)^{-}}({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)" "\\lim_{b\\to (-3)^{-}}\\left({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186348798 :answer/value "{2}^{3/2}(1+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})" "{2}^{3/2}\\left(1+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186348811 :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186348944 :answer/value "{2}^{1/2}(1-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2})" "{2}^{1/2}\\left(1-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186349012 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-\\frac{1}{60}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{3}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{3}-\\frac{1}{60}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{5}+⋯"] [:db/cas 17592186349048 :answer/value "\\lim_{b\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{b}^{-3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)" "\\lim_{b\\to -\\infty }\\left({\\int }_{b}^{-3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186349212 :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}" "1-\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186349252 :answer/value "1+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}" "1+2{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186349343 :answer/value "(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9}{2})" "\\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186349404 :answer/value "1+{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}" "1+{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186349442 :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2},\