 [:db/cas 17592186350498 :answer/value "\\lim_{b\\to (-6)^{+}}({\\int }_{b}^{-3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)" "\\lim_{b\\to (-6)^{+}}\\left({\\int }_{b}^{-3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186350618 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{6}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-\\frac{1}{120}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{6}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{3}-\\frac{1}{120}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{5}+⋯"] [:db/cas 17592186350685 :answer/value "-1+\\frac{1}{2}{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}" "-1+\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}{\\left(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186350702 :answer/value "\\lim_{b\\to {3}^{-}}({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)" "\\lim_{b\\to {3}^{-}}\\left({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186351164 :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &=2{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=4(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-7{b}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7({c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7{c}_{1}+14{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-7{c}_{1}+\\frac{29}{2}{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ \\frac{29}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$" "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &=2{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=4\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}\\right)+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-7{b}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7({c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7{c}_{1}+14{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-7{c}_{1}+\\frac{29}{2}{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ \\frac{29}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351165 :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=3{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &={b}_{1}+6{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}={c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-5{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-5({c}_{2}-2{c}_{1})+2(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-5{c}_{2}+10{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{32}{3} \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.$" "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=3{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &={b}_{1}+6{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6\\left(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}\\right)\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}={c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-5{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-5({c}_{2}-2{c}_{1})+2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}\\right) \\\\  & =-5{c}_{2}+10{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{32}{3} \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351171 :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &={b}_{1}-{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6{b}_{1}-{b}_{2} \\\\  & =6(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})-(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{8}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$" "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} &={b}_{1}-{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}\\right)+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6{b}_{1}-{b}_{2} \\\\  & =6\\left(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{8}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351189 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({({t}^{2})}^{2}+t)\\cdot 2t+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+t)\\cdot 2t+3{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{5}+5{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5(1)^{3}}{3}+(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}+1]-[0] \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({({t}^{2})}^{2}+t)\\cdot 2t+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+t)\\cdot 2t+3{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{5}+5{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{(1)^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5(1)^{3}}{3}+(1)^{2}\\right]-[0] \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}+1\\right]-[0] \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351193 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{3x/2}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $\\frac{3}{2}$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2}-3(\\alpha (2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2})+\\frac{9}{4}(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}) & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}-6x-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{3x/2} & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{3x/2} & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0 \\\\ {\\left(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{3x/2}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $\\frac{3}{2}$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha \\left(2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\right){e}^{3x/2},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha \\left(2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}\\right){e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\left(2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}\\right){e}^{3x/2}-3\\left(\\alpha \\left(2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\right){e}^{3x/2}\\right)+\\frac{9}{4}(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}) & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ \\left(2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}-6x-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}\\right)\\alpha {e}^{3x/2} & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{3x/2} & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351200 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{25y}{4}=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{2}$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha {e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{-x}-5(-\\alpha {e}^{-x})+\\frac{25}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ (1+5+\\frac{25}{4})\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{16}{49}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$" "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{25y}{4}=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {\\left(\\lambda -\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{2}$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha {e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{-x}-5(-\\alpha {e}^{-x})+\\frac{25}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\left(1+5+\\frac{25}{4}\\right)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{16}{49}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351208 :question/problem "What is the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" "What is the coefficient of ${\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351208 :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351215 :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dt \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi -0 \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\left(-y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dt \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi -0 \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351216 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-x.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot 1+t(2t-1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-x.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot 1+t(2t-1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}\\right]-[0] \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351218 :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (1+2t)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{e}^{2t}-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t))dt \\\\  & ={[{e}^{2t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{e}^{2(1)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (1))]-[{e}^{2(0)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (0))] \\\\  & =[{e}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)]-[1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{2}+0]-[1+0] \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left({e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (1+2t)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(2{e}^{2t}-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t\\right)\\right)dt \\\\  & ={\\left[{e}^{2t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t\\right)\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left[{e}^{2(1)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (1)\\right)\\right]-\\left[{e}^{2(0)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (0)\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\left[{e}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right]-\\left[1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =[{e}^{2}+0]-[1+0] \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351232 :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{3}{4}\\alpha -\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(\\alpha -\\frac{3}{4}\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}\\alpha -\\beta &=25\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\alpha -\\frac{3}{4}\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-12$ and $\\beta =-16.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\alpha -\\beta \\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\left(\\alpha -\\frac{3}{4}\\beta \\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}\\alpha -\\beta &=25\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\alpha -\\frac{3}{4}\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-12$ and $\\beta =-16.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351239 :question/worked-solution "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(a)}{4!}(x-a)^{4}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ so\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and ${f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}\\right)=-2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}\\right)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}_{4}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =1+(0)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\left(\\frac{(-2)}{2!}\\right){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{0}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{8}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =1-{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(a)}{4!}(x-a)^{4}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ so\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{3!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{4!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and ${f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}\\right)=-2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}\\right)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}_{4}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =1+(0)\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{(-2)}{2!}\\right){\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{0}{3!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{3}+\\frac{8}{4!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =1-{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351246 :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2t+{t}^{2}-1+1}{t}+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+t+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[2t+\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[2(2)+\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[4+6] \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2t+{t}^{2}-1+1}{t}+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+t+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\left[2t+\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\left[2(2)+\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{2}\\right]-[0] \\\\  & =[4+6] \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351249 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in \\left[\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right].$"] [:db/cas 17592186351249 :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}(y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){|}_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\left(y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-{\\sin }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){|}_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351250 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi {t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\pi t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2{t}^{2}}\\right)\\cdot 1+3t\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & =[t+2\\pi {t}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[2+2\\pi (2)^{3}]-[1+2\\pi (1)^{3}] \\\\  & =[2+16\\pi ]-[1+2\\pi ] \\\\  & =1+14\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\pi {t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\pi t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2{t}^{2}}\\right)\\cdot 1+3t\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & =[t+2\\pi {t}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[2+2\\pi (2)^{3}]-[1+2\\pi (1)^{3}] \\\\  & =[2+16\\pi ]-[1+2\\pi ] \\\\  & =1+14\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351255 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-4\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+4\\text{i})(-2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $\\det (A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-4\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}\\right){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}\\right){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2+4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2-4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+4\\text{i})(-2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351267 :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e9d2238ca77532c3adda8d35f94061d108c3e15432ce48fdc33885ddf4f4e48.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" "![](images/6e/6e9d2238ca77532c3adda8d35f94061d108c3e15432ce48fdc33885ddf4f4e48.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $\\left(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351267 :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=5.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $5,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $5.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$" "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $\\left(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right),$ we must have $r=5.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $5,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $5.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186351268 :question/problem "![](images/a6/a615f7a80b81f89fccd8c82bf65d65e3a4a65958ec8a6fbc746ae68749949a87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and $m∠AOB=\\pi ,$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" "![](images/a6/a615f7a80b81f89fccd8c82bf65d65e3a4a65958ec8a6fbc746ae68749949a87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $\\left(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)$ and $m∠AOB=\\pi ,$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351268 :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$" "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $\\left(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186351270 :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=2{b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2} &=3{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}+8{b}_{2} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})+8(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=2{b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2} &=3{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}\\right)\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}+8{b}_{2} \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}\\right)+8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351275 :question/worked-solution "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{3-4y{x}^{3}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}(\\frac{3}{y}-4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{3}{y}\\text{ }\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}4{x}^{3}\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{3}{y}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(2-1){\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{3}{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}4{x}^{3}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(e-1){\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(e-1)[{x}^{4}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(e-1)({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =15(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{3-4y{x}^{3}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A=3-15(e-1)=18-15e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{3-4y{x}^{3}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{R}\\left(\\frac{3}{y}-4{x}^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{R}\\frac{3}{y}\\text{ }\\text{d}A-{∬}_{R}4{x}^{3}\\text{ }\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{3}{y}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(2-1){\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{3}{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}4{x}^{3}\\text{ }\\text{d}A & =(e-1){\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(e-1)[{x}^{4}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(e-1)({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =15(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}\\frac{3-4y{x}^{3}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A=3-15(e-1)=18-15e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351279 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(1,9)\\times (-1,1)$ and ${R}_{2}=(1,9)\\times (1,4).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =(9-1){\\int }_{-1}^{1}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+({\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =8[{y}^{3}]_{-1}^{1}+[\\sqrt{x}]_{1}^{9}\\cdot [\\sqrt{y}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =8({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3})+(3-1)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that we have $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2}$, where ${R}_{1}=(1,9)\\times (-1,1)$ and ${R}_{2}=(1,9)\\times (1,4).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A= & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{R}_{1}}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{R}_{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =(9-1){\\int }_{-1}^{1}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+\\left({\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x\\right)\\left({\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y\\right) \\\\  & =8[{y}^{3}]_{-1}^{1}+[\\sqrt{x}]_{1}^{9}\\cdot [\\sqrt{y}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =8({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3})+(3-1)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351284 :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-1 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5})\\cup (\\frac{1}{5},\\infty ).\n$$" "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-1 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in \\left(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\cup \\left(\\frac{1}{5},\\infty \\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351285 :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-7}{kx-4}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\infty ,\\frac{4}{5})\\cup (\\frac{4}{5},\\infty ).\n$$ What is the value of $k?$" "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-7}{kx-4}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\infty ,\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\cup \\left(\\frac{4}{5},\\infty \\right).\n$$ What is the value of $k?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351288 :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}[x]_{0}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}(y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}[x]_{0}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}(y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\left[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351289 :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{3}-1)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{y}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+\\frac{1}{4})-(\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{3}-1)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\left(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\left(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,dy & ={\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{y}\\right]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\left(8+\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+1\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351294 :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-x}^{3x}\\sqrt{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-x}^{3x}\\sqrt{x+y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{2}{3}(x+y)^{3/2}]}_{-x}^{3x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{3}(x+3x)^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{3}(x-x)^{3/2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}(4x)^{3/2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{16}{3}{x}^{3/2}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3/2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3/2}\\,dx & =\\frac{16}{3}{[\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}(\\frac{2}{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-x}^{3x}\\sqrt{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-x}^{3x}\\sqrt{x+y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[\\frac{2}{3}(x+y)^{3/2}\\right]}_{-x}^{3x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}(x+3x)^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{3}(x-x)^{3/2}\\right)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}(4x)^{3/2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{16}{3}{x}^{3/2}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3/2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3/2}\\,dx & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\left[\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}-0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351295 :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x{[{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx & ={[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(64-24)-(1-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =40+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x{[{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx & ={\\left[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(64-24)-\\left(1-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =40+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351296 :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/aa/aa635ca5ef3198fea56d684b034de476a72e4ace2a4c51b37c6c59977036eb76.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=e$ intercepts the curve $y={e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le e.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/b7/b78021a06c9fea100b75c66a619c425345cda3666d84da018895be15a53af06d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]}_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/aa/aa635ca5ef3198fea56d684b034de476a72e4ace2a4c51b37c6c59977036eb76.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=e$ intercepts the curve $y={e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le e.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/b7/b78021a06c9fea100b75c66a619c425345cda3666d84da018895be15a53af06d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\left[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\right]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]}_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={\\left[\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡x}{2}\\right]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351305 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}} \\\\ 9-{y}^{2} & =3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 3.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/91/91f377562a1e94218b7be886cd63638e8881b0c4fac231a810bb6bff3a1fe643.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}(9-{y}^{2}-(3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}))\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{2}{[6y-\\frac{2}{9}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(18-6-0) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}} \\\\ 9-{y}^{2} & =3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 3.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/91/91f377562a1e94218b7be886cd63638e8881b0c4fac231a810bb6bff3a1fe643.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}\\left(9-{y}^{2}-\\left(3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\left(6-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}\\right)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\left(6-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}\\right)\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\left[6y-\\frac{2}{9}{y}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(18-6-0) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351313 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=3.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$ At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=3.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351313 :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{6}].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\frac{7}{2}+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3+([\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]-[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]) \\\\  & =3+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =3+(0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$" "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in \\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\frac{7}{2}+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+{\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right]}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3+\\left(\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(0-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right]\\right) \\\\  & =3+\\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =3+\\left(0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351319 :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+{90}^{∘})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}+{90}^{∘}\\right)?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186351320 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186351322 :question/problem "Consider the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Consider the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186351323 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=7\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{\\pi }{12}).\n$$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=7\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351326 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186351326 :question/worked-solution "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 6x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{n\\pi }{6},$ where $n$ is an integer." "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $\\left(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\n6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 6x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{n\\pi }{6},$ where $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186351328 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function  $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function  $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186351328 :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer." "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186351334 :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})+5$ at its local minimum points?" "What is the value of $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)+5$ at its local minimum points?"] [:db/cas 17592186351334 :question/worked-solution "On a positive branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$ are local minimums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nAdding $5$ to the above inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12})+5\\ge 1+5 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=6$ at the local minimum points." "On a positive branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$ are local minimums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nAdding $5$ to the above inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(7x-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)+5\\ge 1+5 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=6$ at the local minimum points."] [:db/cas 17592186351344 :question/worked-solution "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{⁗}(a)}{4!}(x-a)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ so\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and ${f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}_{4}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =-1+0(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{0}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{4!}\\right){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{⁗}(a)}{4!}(x-a)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ so\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{3!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{4!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ ${f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and ${f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}_{4}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =-1+0\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{0}{3!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{4!}\\right){\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351352 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-6{e}^{-2t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t})+C \\\\  & =3{e}^{-2t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 2 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3{e}^{-2t}-1.$" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-6{e}^{-2t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t}\\right)+C \\\\  & =3{e}^{-2t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 2 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3{e}^{-2t}-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351354 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (9{e}^{-3t}-4{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-4(-{e}^{-t})+C \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3t}+4{e}^{-t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-3{e}^{0}+4{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 1 & =-3+4+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}.$" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (9{e}^{-3t}-4{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\right)-4(-{e}^{-t})+C \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3t}+4{e}^{-t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-3{e}^{0}+4{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 1 & =-3+4+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351355 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 3 & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+C \\\\ 3 & =-2\\cdot 0+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\right)+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+C \\\\ 3 & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+C \\\\ 3 & =-2\\cdot 0+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351357 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ where $$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$" "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ where $$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\left(12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351357 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ g(y) & =\\frac{1}{y}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=\\frac{1}{y}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C.\n$$" "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\left(12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ g(y) & =\\frac{1}{y}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=\\frac{1}{y}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351361 :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.  \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=xy$ and $Q=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=4x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=-4x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F=xy\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$ only." "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.  \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=xy$ and $Q=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=4x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=-4x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F=xy\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\right)\\,j$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186351369 :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[y]_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[y]_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx & ={\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351370 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the curve with the line $y=0.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/db/db0dd47348fb2f9a97c9e0514f74adae05830de7e03f1aea2886eaa95701200e.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the curve with the line $y=0.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/db/db0dd47348fb2f9a97c9e0514f74adae05830de7e03f1aea2886eaa95701200e.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx & ={\\left[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351371 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the line $y=x$ with the line $y=2.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/92/92b1eda4ccf9e62e1f7310b1ba9bef5cc6c434755e74196d1829a30f2c4a3c53.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{8x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the line $y=x$ with the line $y=2.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/92/92b1eda4ccf9e62e1f7310b1ba9bef5cc6c434755e74196d1829a30f2c4a3c53.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\left[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\,dx & ={\\left[\\frac{8x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351382 :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\,\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})(x+\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{36}{5}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5}) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+36 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\,\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)\\left(x+\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{36}{5}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\left({x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+36 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351404 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/c9/c9072d2b8f1b49770403280af13d3d8e681247f168e796c056eb697fe28b8ba9.png)\n\n  \n\nThe  region $D={(x,y)|\\,\\,|x-1|+|y|\\le 1}$ can be  partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/d1/d16296029378c7adb891affba63683971ff972b5ed0268641ac3b677c694b5f8.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-y}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,-y\\le x\\le y+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{|x-1|+|y|\\le 1}6(x+y)^{2}\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}6(x+y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}6(x+y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}6(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-y}^{y+2}6(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[3(x+y)^{2}]_{y}^{2-y}\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}[3(x+y)^{2}]_{-y}^{y+2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3(4-4{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}3(2y+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{[4y-\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}+4{[(y+1)^{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =8+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's draw a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/c9/c9072d2b8f1b49770403280af13d3d8e681247f168e796c056eb697fe28b8ba9.png)\n\n  \n\nThe  region $D={(x,y)|\\,\\,|x-1|+|y|\\le 1}$ can be  partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/d1/d16296029378c7adb891affba63683971ff972b5ed0268641ac3b677c694b5f8.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-y}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,-y\\le x\\le y+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{|x-1|+|y|\\le 1}6(x+y)^{2}\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}6(x+y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}6(x+y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}6(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-y}^{y+2}6(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[3(x+y)^{2}]_{y}^{2-y}\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}[3(x+y)^{2}]_{-y}^{y+2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3(4-4{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}3(2y+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{\\left[4y-\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1}+4{[(y+1)^{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =8+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351405 :question/worked-solution "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/f2/f26c3e1bbbc268308db8d419bc799ad5349673e97c8828471997a5d36cb94d7d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le x+1\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le 3-x\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2y\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}2y\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}2y\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(x-1)^{2}}^{x+1}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{(x-1)^{2}}^{3-x}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{|}_{(x-1)^{2}}^{x+1}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}{|}_{(x-1)^{2}}^{3-x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(x+1)^{2}-(x-1)^{4}]\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}[(3-x)^{2}-(x-1)^{4}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(x-1)^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1}+{[-\\frac{(3-x)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(x-1)^{5}}{5}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =((\\frac{8}{3}-0)-(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}))+((-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})-(-\\frac{8}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}+\\frac{32}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/f2/f26c3e1bbbc268308db8d419bc799ad5349673e97c8828471997a5d36cb94d7d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le x+1\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le 3-x\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2y\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}2y\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}2y\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(x-1)^{2}}^{x+1}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{(x-1)^{2}}^{3-x}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}{|}_{(x-1)^{2}}^{x+1}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}{|}_{(x-1)^{2}}^{3-x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(x+1)^{2}-(x-1)^{4}]\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}[(3-x)^{2}-(x-1)^{4}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(x-1)^{5}}{5}\\right]}_{0}^{1}+{\\left[-\\frac{(3-x)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(x-1)^{5}}{5}\\right]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{8}{3}-0\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\right)+\\left(\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}-0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}+\\frac{32}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351411 :question/worked-solution "![](images/fb/fb111bd5365ad339dcddbf5e3d98b5e53cfc029ed88c3785154194f1e64d3297.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0b/0bed4692a7a6851ef2b66cb3842ebac6b2ef7ef4ff30bed41e84b7cb71c4f5c0.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})}{4}[{x}^{4}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3})]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/fb/fb111bd5365ad339dcddbf5e3d98b5e53cfc029ed88c3785154194f1e64d3297.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/0b/0bed4692a7a6851ef2b66cb3842ebac6b2ef7ef4ff30bed41e84b7cb71c4f5c0.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})}{4}[{x}^{4}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\left[-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3})\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351412 :question/worked-solution "![](images/d5/d5d777577f85cd577ce35bfd3a99c1e2218622d1856f4fe7ee4afe1cfd2c9f86.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 8,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le x\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. Its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05ec37a8f4fd33c05a2438df5c5fbc697f8b560fde0b9fe73922a0db56a09c2c.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}[y]_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{{x}^{4}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{16}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/d5/d5d777577f85cd577ce35bfd3a99c1e2218622d1856f4fe7ee4afe1cfd2c9f86.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 8,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le x\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. Its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05ec37a8f4fd33c05a2438df5c5fbc697f8b560fde0b9fe73922a0db56a09c2c.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}[y]_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{16}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351414 :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+5t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-4{t}^{2}+t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6{t}^{2}+6t+5,\\,8{t}^{3}-8t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}+6t+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}-8t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12t+6,\\,24{t}^{2}-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=0,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =⟨12(0)+6,\\,24(0)-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(0)|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(0)| & =|⟨6,\\,-8⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+5t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-4{t}^{2}+t+1)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨6{t}^{2}+6t+5,\\,8{t}^{3}-8t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}+6t+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}-8t+1)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨12t+6,\\,24{t}^{2}-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=0,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =⟨12(0)+6,\\,24(0)-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(0)|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(0)| & =|⟨6,\\,-8⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351415 :question/problem "The position vector $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{4}{1+t},\\,\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-4t+5⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$" "The position vector $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nr=\\left\\langle \\frac{4}{1+t},\\,\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-4t+5\\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351415 :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{4}{1+t}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-4t+5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{(1+t)^{2}},\\,{t}^{2}-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{4}{(1+t)^{2}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{8}{(1+t)^{3}},\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨\\frac{8}{(1+(1))^{3}},\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{8}{{2}^{3}},\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨1,\\,2⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{4}{1+t}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-4t+5\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{4}{(1+t)^{2}},\\,{t}^{2}-4\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{4}{(1+t)^{2}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-4)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{8}{(1+t)^{3}},\\,2t\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{8}{(1+(1))^{3}},\\,2(1)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{8}{{2}^{3}},\\,2\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨1,\\,2⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351418 :question/worked-solution "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/2f/2fcb30778db2f051a7a819ae701808af59ac18d27c25a9c57ce678caa6c3ee3b.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le -x\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}2x\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}2x\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{-x}2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x}2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}2xy{|}_{{x}^{2}}^{-x}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy{|}_{{x}^{2}}^{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}2x[-x-{x}^{2}]\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x[x-{x}^{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}]}_{-1}^{0}+{[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =((0-(-\\frac{2}{3}(-1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{4}))+((\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{4})-0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/2f/2fcb30778db2f051a7a819ae701808af59ac18d27c25a9c57ce678caa6c3ee3b.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le -x\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}2x\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}2x\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{-x}2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x}2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}2xy{|}_{{x}^{2}}^{-x}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy{|}_{{x}^{2}}^{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}2x[-x-{x}^{2}]\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x[x-{x}^{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\right]}_{-1}^{0}+{\\left[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(0-\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}(-1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{4}\\right)\\right)+\\left(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{4}\\right)-0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351448 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=2\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right)\n$$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351448 :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =2(0+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =2(0+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351449 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=2\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right)\n$$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351449 :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}+\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351450 :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(2-3x)^{3/2} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =(2-0)^{3/2}={2}^{3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-3x)^{1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-0)^{1/2}=-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{27}{4}(2-3x)^{-1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={2}^{3/2}+(-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2})x+(27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2})\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{3/2}(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(2-3x)^{3/2} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =(2-0)^{3/2}={2}^{3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-3x)^{1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-0)^{1/2}=-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{27}{4}(2-3x)^{-1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={2}^{3/2}+(-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2})x+(27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2})\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{3/2}\\left(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351451 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-2,3):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,3]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-2)^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-2,3):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,3]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-2)^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}\\left({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351459 :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the sum rule and write our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,1]\\times [0,2]\\times [-1,1]$ is a rectangular box, and each integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our sum of repeated integrals as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate each of these separately:\n\n- For the first product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & =(1-0)\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we apply the sum rule and write our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,1]\\times [0,2]\\times [-1,1]$ is a rectangular box, and each integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our sum of repeated integrals as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate each of these separately:\n\n- For the first product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z & =(1-0)\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}\\right]}_{0}^{2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351461 :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+2)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x=A(x+2)^{2}+B(x+2)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2}+4(-2) & =A(-2+2)^{2}+B(-2+2)+C \\\\ -4 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+4(0) & =A(0+2)^{2}+B(0+2)+C \\\\ 0 & =4A+2B-4 \\\\ 2A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}+4(-1) & =A(-1+2)^{2}+B(-1+2)+C \\\\ -3 & =A+B-4 \\\\ A+B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the equations $2A+B=2$ and $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =1 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+2)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x=A(x+2)^{2}+B(x+2)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2}+4(-2) & =A(-2+2)^{2}+B(-2+2)+C \\\\ -4 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+4(0) & =A(0+2)^{2}+B(0+2)+C \\\\ 0 & =4A+2B-4 \\\\ 2A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}+4(-1) & =A(-1+2)^{2}+B(-1+2)+C \\\\ -3 & =A+B-4 \\\\ A+B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the equations $2A+B=2$ and $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =1 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{2}{(x+2)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."] [:db/cas 17592186351462 :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+3=A(x-2)^{2}+B(x-2)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2}-3(2)+3 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2-2)+C \\\\ 1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-3(1)+3 & =A(1-2)^{2}+B(1-2)+C \\\\ 1 & =A-B+1 \\\\ A-B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=3$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{2}-3(3)+3 & =A(3-2)^{2}+B(3-2)+C \\\\ 3 & =A+B+1 \\\\ A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=0$ and $A+B=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =0+2 \\\\ 2A & =2 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nand substituting back into $A-B=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-B & =0 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{1}{x-2}-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-2)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+3=A(x-2)^{2}+B(x-2)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2}-3(2)+3 & =A(2-2)^{2}+B(2-2)+C \\\\ 1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-3(1)+3 & =A(1-2)^{2}+B(1-2)+C \\\\ 1 & =A-B+1 \\\\ A-B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=3$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{2}-3(3)+3 & =A(3-2)^{2}+B(3-2)+C \\\\ 3 & =A+B+1 \\\\ A+B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=0$ and $A+B=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =0+2 \\\\ 2A & =2 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nand substituting back into $A-B=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-B & =0 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-\\frac{1}{x-2}-\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."] [:db/cas 17592186351464 :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\hat{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2-\\frac{9}{4})}^{2}+{(1-\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right))}^{2}+{(-3-\\left(-\\frac{7}{4}\\right))}^{2}+{(1-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{49}{16}+\\frac{25}{16}+\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{76}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\hat{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(2-\\frac{9}{4}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(1-\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-3-\\left(-\\frac{7}{4}\\right)\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(1-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{49}{16}+\\frac{25}{16}+\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{76}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351476 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2(-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-\\frac{{e}^{2(0)}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2(-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\right){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-\\frac{{e}^{2(0)}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{2\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351477 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3({e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{3x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\pi }}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\pi }}{10}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{3\\pi }+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3({e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{3x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\left(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\right){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\pi }}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\pi }}{10}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{3\\pi }+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351478 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}\\right){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}))}{10}+\\frac{{e}^{-0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0))}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi /3}}{10}+\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{-\\pi /3}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}I\\right) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{10}{9}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{10}\\right){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /3}\\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right)}{10}+\\frac{{e}^{-0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0))}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi /3}}{10}+\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{-\\pi /3}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351479 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2} & =2{x}^{1/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2}=2{x}^{1/2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2} & =2{x}^{1/2}+C \\\\ {y}^{3/2} & =3{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{3C}{2} \\\\ y & ={(3{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{3C}{2})}^{2/3} \\\\ y & ={(3{x}^{1/2}+K)}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\frac{3C}{2}.$\n\nUse this family on a connected interval where $3\\sqrt{x}+K>0$. This condition preserves the positive square-root branch used before raising both sides to a power. The calculation above works in the stated positive quadrant; $\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{y}$ is valid there." "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2} & =2{x}^{1/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2}=2{x}^{1/2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2} & =2{x}^{1/2}+C \\\\ {y}^{3/2} & =3{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{3C}{2} \\\\ y & ={\\left(3{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{3C}{2}\\right)}^{2/3} \\\\ y & ={(3{x}^{1/2}+K)}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\frac{3C}{2}.$\n\nUse this family on a connected interval where $3\\sqrt{x}+K>0$. This condition preserves the positive square-root branch used before raising both sides to a power. The calculation above works in the stated positive quadrant; $\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{y}$ is valid there."] [:db/cas 17592186351483 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the constant $A.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+3 & =(Ax+1)(x-1)+{x}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}+2+3 & =(A(2)+1)(2-1)+{2}^{2}+4 \\\\ 9 & =2A+1+8 \\\\ 9 & =2A+9 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{0x+1}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the constant $A.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+3 & =(Ax+1)(x-1)+{x}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}+2+3 & =(A(2)+1)(2-1)+{2}^{2}+4 \\\\ 9 & =2A+1+8 \\\\ 9 & =2A+9 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{0x+1}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x-1}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351487 :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(5)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0+(-1)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{0}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2} \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{0}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+\\frac{(-1)}{5!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯ \\\\  & =-(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{6}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-\\frac{1}{120}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(5)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0+(-1)\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{0}{2!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{3!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{3}+\\frac{0}{4!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{4}+\\frac{(-1)}{5!}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{5}+⋯ \\\\  & =-\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{6}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{3}-\\frac{1}{120}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{5}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351490 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD=(-1)^{2}-4(1)(2)=-7<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x+4}{x({x}^{2}-x+2)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}-x+2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x({x}^{2}-x+2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-3x+4=A({x}^{2}-x+2)+(Bx+C)x.\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=2,$ and $C=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x+4}{x({x}^{2}-x+2)}=\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-x+2}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x+4}{x({x}^{2}-x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}-x+2.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-x+2| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x+4}{x({x}^{2}-x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-x+2)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD=(-1)^{2}-4(1)(2)=-7<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x+4}{x({x}^{2}-x+2)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}-x+2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x({x}^{2}-x+2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-3x+4=A({x}^{2}-x+2)+(Bx+C)x.\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=2,$ and $C=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x+4}{x({x}^{2}-x+2)}=\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-x+2}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x+4}{x({x}^{2}-x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-x+2}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-x+2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}-x+2.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-x+2| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}-3x+4}{x({x}^{2}-x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}-x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-x+2)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351497 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD=(-4)^{2}-4(1)(-3)=28\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{28}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-3x-9=A({x}^{2}-4x-3)+(Bx+C)(x+3)\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=1$ and $C=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}=\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}-4x-3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2(x-2)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-4x-3|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-4x-3|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD=(-4)^{2}-4(1)(-3)=28\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{28}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-3x-9=A({x}^{2}-4x-3)+(Bx+C)(x+3)\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=1$ and $C=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}=\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}-4x-3.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2(x-2)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-4x-3|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-9}{(x+3)({x}^{2}-4x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}-4x-3|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351506 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} &=1 \\\\ {t}^{3} &=0 \\\\ 2t &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =2j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} &=1 \\\\ {t}^{3} &=0 \\\\ 2t &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}2)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =2j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351507 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t &=2 \\\\ {t}^{2} &=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(1),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =-4i+2j+4k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4+16} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t &=2 \\\\ {t}^{2} &=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(1),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2,\\,2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 0,\\,2,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =-4i+2j+4k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4+16} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351508 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}i-\\sqrt{2}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}i-\\sqrt{2}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351510 :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\left\\langle -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351515 :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=⟨1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=0,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=⟨1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\left\\langle -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{C}_{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=0,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."] [:db/cas 17592186351516 :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & =20[\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =10{[{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =10({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(0)) \\\\  & =10(1-0) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\left[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}\\right]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & =20\\left[\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =10{[{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =10\\left({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(0)\\right) \\\\  & =10(1-0) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351517 :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}y+y{e}^{x}-y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(\\frac{x}{y}+{e}^{x}-x)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{∇}f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}1+1\\cdot {e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(\\frac{-1}{1}+{e}^{-1}-(-1))\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-1}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-1}\\mathbf{j}.$" "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}y+y{e}^{x}-y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{x}{y}+{e}^{x}-x\\right)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{∇}f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}1+1\\cdot {e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{-1}{1}+{e}^{-1}-(-1)\\right)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-1}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{-1}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351519 :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2})(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{4}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $6.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $6.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}\\right)\\left(x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}\\right)=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{4}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $6.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $6.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\left({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351520 :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi y,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{{y}^{2}}+\\pi x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{1}+\\pi \\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{{1}^{2}}+\\pi \\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +\\pi ,0+\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}g(1,1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+\\pi \\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy\\right),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi y,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{{y}^{2}}+\\pi x\\right\\rangle  \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{1}+\\pi \\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{{1}^{2}}+\\pi \\cdot 1\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +\\pi ,0+\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}g(1,1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\cdot \\left\\langle -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+\\pi \\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351521 :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-1,1⟩?$" "Consider the function $g(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-1,1⟩?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351521 :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}$ is equal to the directional derivative $$\n{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y{\\sec }^{2}⁡x,\\,2x{\\sec }^{2}⁡y-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\,\\pi -2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative $$\n{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\pi -2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(2-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+(\\pi -2)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi -4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}$ is equal to the directional derivative $$\n{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y{\\sec }^{2}⁡x,\\,2x{\\sec }^{2}⁡y-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\left\\langle 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2\\cdot 1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\,\\pi -2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative $$\n{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\n$$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}g\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\pi -2⟩\\cdot \\left\\langle -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{(2-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+(\\pi -2)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi -4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351524 :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩,\\,0\\le s<6\\pi .\n$$" "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=\\left\\langle -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle ,\\,0\\le s<6\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351524 :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3},\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3}|\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3},\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left\\langle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3}|\\sqrt{{\\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351526 :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+5x-3$ has a root in the interval $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+5x-3$ has a root in the interval $\\left(0,\\frac{3}{2}\\right),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186351526 :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\pm 1,\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ are $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $1.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{1}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-3$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n|  | $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{1}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{2})(2{x}^{2}+2x+6).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+2x+6$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(2)(6) \\\\  & =4-48 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\pm 1,\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $\\left(0,\\frac{3}{2}\\right),$ are $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $1.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-3$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n|  | $2$ | $2$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(2{x}^{2}+2x+6).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+2x+6$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(2)(6) \\\\  & =4-48 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186351528 :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\pi $ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩.\n$$" "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=\\pi $ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\left\\langle -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351528 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature: \n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left\\langle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left\\langle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left\\langle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351529 :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨1,\\,t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\frac{t}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨1,\\,t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\left\\langle -\\frac{t}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{2})^{3/2}}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351537 :question/problem "What is the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{4}?$" "What is the coefficient of ${\\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{4}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186351537 :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)(x-\\frac{1}{4})+\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=-{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4})=-{\\pi }^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{2} & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{2}\\right)}{2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{2}{\\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${\\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=-{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\right)=-{\\pi }^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${\\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{2} & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{2}\\right)}{2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351545 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${\\mathbf{u}}_{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{2},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{3}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{4} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and first equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-4(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4})+2{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{4}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=3$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=\\frac{-1}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${\\mathbf{u}}_{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{2},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\mathbf{u}}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{u}}_{1}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{2}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0 \\\\ {\\mathbf{u}}_{3}\\cdot \\mathbf{x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{4} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}-2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and first equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-4\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4}\\right)+2{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{4}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=3$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=\\frac{-1}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186351552 :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},0)\\,.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $$\n\\displaystyle {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$  So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/f5/f518a206638071a7b8548882a453123b6d78aed0aa94cc0e6e23429f22c6f1b3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\le r\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta /2}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta /2}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta /2}^{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}(2\\theta )]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We 