=0.25{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tool is released with an initial velocity of $2.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=2.5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =4\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}g\\cdot 0+C\\right) \\\\ 2.5 & =4\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0.625 \\\\ C & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.625)≈0.733\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=4\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}gt+0.733\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363862 :answer/value "v(t)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}gt+0.625)" "v(t)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}gt+0.625\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186363863 :answer/value "v(t)=4\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}gt+0.733)" "v(t)=4\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}gt+0.733\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186363867 :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the block: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=15g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2}=-1.47{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the block is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=15g-1.47{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 15g-1.47{v}^{2} & =15\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-0.098{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.01{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.01{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.01{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.1v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.1}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.1v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.1v) & =\\frac{1}{10}gt+C \\\\ 0.1v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{10}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{10}gt+C),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=0.1{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the block is released with an initial velocity of $1\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=1,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{10}g\\cdot 0+C) \\\\ 1 & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0.1 \\\\ C & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.1)≈0.100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{10}gt+0.100)\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of the block after $t=1$ second is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 9.8\\cdot 1+0.100) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(0.98+0.100) \\\\  & =7.931\\,99… \\\\  & ≈7.93\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "First, note that only two forces act on the block: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=15g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2}=-1.47{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the block is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=15g-1.47{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 15g-1.47{v}^{2} & =15\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-0.098{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.01{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.01{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.01{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.1v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.1}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.1v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.1v) & =\\frac{1}{10}gt+C \\\\ 0.1v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{10}gt+C\\right) \\\\ v(t) & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{10}gt+C\\right),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=0.1{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the block is released with an initial velocity of $1\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=1,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{10}g\\cdot 0+C\\right) \\\\ 1 & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0.1 \\\\ C & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.1)≈0.100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{10}gt+0.100\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of the block after $t=1$ second is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 9.8\\cdot 1+0.100\\right) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(0.98+0.100) \\\\  & =7.931\\,99… \\\\  & ≈7.93\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186363874 :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the structure: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=2500g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2}=-39.2{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the structure is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=2500g-39.2{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 2500g-39.2{v}^{2} & =2500\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-0.01568{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.0016{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.0016{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.0016{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.04v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.04}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.04v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.04v) & =\\frac{1}{25}gt+C \\\\ 0.04v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}gt+C),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=0.04{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the structure is released with an initial velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=4,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}g\\cdot 0+C) \\\\ 4 & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0.16 \\\\ C & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.16)≈0.161\\,38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}gt+0.161\\,38)\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of the structure after $t=1$ second is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}\\cdot 9.8\\cdot 1+0.161\\,38) \\\\  & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(0.392+0.161\\,38) \\\\  & =12.576\\,29… \\\\  & ≈12.58\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "First, note that only two forces act on the structure: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=2500g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2}=-39.2{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the structure is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=2500g-39.2{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 2500g-39.2{v}^{2} & =2500\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-0.01568{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.0016{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.0016{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.0016{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.04v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.04}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.04v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.04v) & =\\frac{1}{25}gt+C \\\\ 0.04v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{25}gt+C\\right) \\\\ v(t) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{25}gt+C\\right),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=0.04{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the structure is released with an initial velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=4,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{25}g\\cdot 0+C\\right) \\\\ 4 & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0.16 \\\\ C & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.16)≈0.161\\,38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{25}gt+0.161\\,38\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of the structure after $t=1$ second is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{25}\\cdot 9.8\\cdot 1+0.161\\,38\\right) \\\\  & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(0.392+0.161\\,38) \\\\  & =12.576\\,29… \\\\  & ≈12.58\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186363891 :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the sack: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=50g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the sack is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=50g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 50g-k{v}^{2} & =50\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-\\frac{k}{50}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the sack's velocity is $2.5\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values and $g=9.8$ into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n7.35=g-\\frac{k}{50}\\cdot {2.5}^{2}\\,⟹\\,k=19.6\\,\\text{kg/m}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-0.392{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.04{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.04{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.04{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.2v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.2}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.2v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.2v) & =\\frac{1}{5}gt+C \\\\ 0.2v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}gt+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=0.2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the body is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=0,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}g\\cdot 0+C) \\\\ 0 & =5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}gt)\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $\\frac{1}{5}g,$ we have\n\n$$\nv(t)=5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(1.96t).\n$$" "First, note that only two forces act on the sack: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=50g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the sack is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=50g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order