nd negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the container is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=1000g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 1000g-k{v}^{2} & =1000\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-\\frac{k}{1000}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the container's velocity is $25\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n7.35=g-\\frac{k}{1000}\\cdot (25)^{2}\\,⟹\\,k=3.92\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-0.00392{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.0004{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.0004{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.0004{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.02v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.02}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.02v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.02v) & =\\frac{1}{50}gt+C \\\\ 0.02v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{50}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =50(\\frac{1}{50}gt+C),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=0.02{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the container is released with an initial velocity of $25\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=25,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =50(\\frac{1}{50}g\\cdot 0+C) \\\\ 25 & =50\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ 0.5 & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ C & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.5)≈0.549\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)≈50\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{50}gt+0.549)\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $\\frac{1}{50}g,$ we have\n\n$$\nv(t)=50\\operatorna